/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/ai-200.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("ai-200", [["az1",0,1,"A team builds a Python API image on every commit. Developers do not have Docker installed, and the build must run in Azure. Which command should the pipeline run?",["az acr build --registry myreg --image api:{{.Run.ID}} .","docker build -t myreg.azurecr.io/api .","az acr import --name myreg --source api:latest","az containerapp up --source . --ingress internal"],0,"az acr build is an ACR quick task: it uploads the context and builds and pushes the image in Azure without a local Docker engine. docker build needs local Docker, and az acr import copies an existing image rather than building one.","Skills 1: ACR",[null,"docker build runs on a local Docker engine, which the developers do not have, and it does not push the image to ACR.","az acr import copies an image that already exists in another registry; it does not build anything from source.","az containerapp up builds and deploys a Container App in one step; it is not the registry build step the pipeline needs, and internal ingress is irrelevant."],2],["az2",0,1,"A company wants all its container images rebuilt automatically when Microsoft publishes a patched Python base image. What should the developer configure?",["A Container Apps scale rule on image age","An ACR task with a base image update trigger","A retention policy for untagged manifests","Geo-replication of the registry to a second Azure region"],1,"ACR Tasks can track the base image in a Dockerfile and rebuild the app image when that base image is updated. Retention policies and geo-replication do not rebuild anything.","Skills 1: ACR Tasks",["Container Apps scale rules change replica counts based on load or events; there is no rule on image age and scaling never rebuilds images.",null,"A retention policy deletes untagged manifests after a number of days; it cleans up storage but never triggers a rebuild.","Geo-replication copies the registry's content to other regions for faster pulls; it does not watch base images or rebuild."],2],["az3",0,1,"A registry must be reachable only through a private endpoint in a virtual network and replicated to a second region. Which ACR tier is required?",["Basic","Standard","Premium","Any tier with admin user enabled"],2,"Private endpoints and geo-replication are Premium features. Basic and Standard differ in storage and throughput but do not offer these.","Skills 1: ACR",["Basic is the entry tier for development with lower storage and throughput; it supports neither private endpoints nor geo-replication.","Standard adds storage and throughput over Basic but still lacks private endpoints and geo-replication.",null,"The admin user is an authentication option available on every tier; it has nothing to do with private endpoints or replication."],1],["az4",0,1,"A Container App must pull images from ACR without any stored password. What is the recommended setup?",["Enable the ACR admin user and store its password as a Container Apps secret","Make the registry repository public for the app's IP range","Create a service principal and paste its secret into an environment variable","Give the app's managed identity the AcrPull role on the registry"],3,"A managed identity with AcrPull removes stored secrets and grants only pull rights. The admin user and service principal both leave a secret to store and rotate.","Skills 1: ACR authentication",["The admin user is one shared, full-rights username and password; storing it as a secret still leaves a credential to protect and rotate.","ACR repositories cannot be made public for an IP range, and anonymous pull would drop authentication rather than use an identity.","A service principal secret in an environment variable is still a stored password that can leak and must be rotated.",null],2],["az5",0,1,"Operations wants production to run exactly the image that passed testing, even if someone later pushes a new image with the same tag. How should the image be referenced?",["By the latest tag","By its manifest digest","By the repository name only","By a tag that includes the registry name"],1,"A digest (sha256:...) identifies one immutable manifest. Tags, including latest, can be moved to point at a different image.","Skills 1: Tags and digests",["latest is a movable tag that any later push can repoint, which is exactly the risk described.",null,"A repository name alone resolves to the latest tag by default, so it gives no guarantee about which image runs.","Adding the registry name only makes the reference fully qualified; the tag part can still be moved to a new image."],3],["az6",0,1,"A custom Linux container on App Service listens on port 8000, and the site returns errors because the platform cannot reach the app. Which app setting fixes this?",["WEBSITES_PORT=8000","WEBSITE_RUN_FROM_PACKAGE=1","SCM_DO_BUILD_DURING_DEPLOYMENT=true","FUNCTIONS_WORKER_RUNTIME=python"],0,"WEBSITES_PORT tells App Service which container port to forward traffic to. The other settings control package deployment, build on deploy and Functions runtime.","Skills 1: App Service containers",[null,"WEBSITE_RUN_FROM_PACKAGE mounts a zip package as the site content; it is for code deployments, not for container port mapping.","SCM_DO_BUILD_DURING_DEPLOYMENT tells the Kudu/Oryx build to run on code deploy; it does not tell App Service which port the container uses.","FUNCTIONS_WORKER_RUNTIME selects the language worker for Azure Functions and has no effect on a custom container web app's port."],1],["az7",0,1,"A team wants to send 10% of users to a new version of a Container App while 90% stay on the current one. What must they configure?",["Single revision mode with a new image tag","A KEDA HTTP scale rule with 10 concurrent requests","Multiple revision mode with traffic weights on each revision","Two Container Apps environments behind one shared external ingress endpoint"],2,"Traffic splitting between revisions needs multiple revision mode, where each active revision gets a percentage. Single revision mode moves all traffic to the newest revision.","Skills 1: Container Apps revisions",["Single revision mode moves all traffic to the newest revision once it is ready, so a 90/10 split is not possible.","A KEDA HTTP scale rule changes the number of replicas based on concurrent requests; it does not divide users between versions.",null,"Two environments would be two separate network boundaries; Container Apps does not share one ingress endpoint across environments for weighted splitting."],2],["az8",0,1,"A background worker in Container Apps reads a Service Bus queue. It should run zero replicas when the queue is empty and add replicas as the backlog grows. What should be configured?",["An HTTP scale rule with min replicas 1 and max replicas 10 on the worker","A custom azure-servicebus scale rule with min replicas 0","A CPU scale rule with min replicas 0","A fixed replica count of 5"],1,"The KEDA azure-servicebus scaler scales on queue length and can scale to zero when min replicas is 0. CPU rules cannot scale to zero, and HTTP rules only react to incoming requests.","Skills 1: Container Apps scaling",["An HTTP rule reacts to incoming requests, which a queue worker does not receive, and min replicas 1 prevents scaling to zero.",null,"CPU and memory scale rules cannot scale an app to zero, because they need a running replica to measure usage.","A fixed count of 5 replicas never reacts to the backlog and keeps billing even when the queue is empty."],3],["az9",0,1,"An internal API in Container Apps must be callable only by other apps in the same environment, not from the internet. How should ingress be set?",["Disable ingress and use Dapr only","External ingress with IP restrictions","Internal ingress","External ingress on port 443 with a custom domain"],2,"Internal ingress exposes the app only inside the Container Apps environment (and its virtual network). External ingress is reachable from the internet even with extra restrictions.","Skills 1: Container Apps ingress",["Disabling ingress removes the HTTP endpoint entirely, so other apps could not call the API over HTTP; Dapr is optional and not required for this.","External ingress is still published to the internet; IP restrictions only filter callers and do not make the app internal.",null,"External ingress with a custom domain on 443 is explicitly internet-facing, the opposite of the requirement."],1],["az10",0,1,"A nightly batch step should run in a container at 02:00, finish, and stop billing until the next run. Which Container Apps feature fits best?",["A scheduled Container Apps job","A Container App with min replicas 1","An App Service WebJob in a custom container","A second revision with 0% traffic"],0,"Container Apps jobs run to completion; a scheduled job uses a cron expression and does not keep a replica running between runs. A Container App with min replicas 1 bills all day.","Skills 1: Container Apps jobs",[null,"A Container App with min replicas 1 keeps a replica running (and billed) all day even when there is no work.","WebJobs run inside an App Service plan that is billed continuously, and they are not the Container Apps feature for run-to-completion tasks.","A revision with 0% traffic still exists as a running app revision; traffic weight does not schedule work or stop billing."],2],["az11",0,1,"An AKS Deployment's pods are stuck with ErrImagePull when pulling from a private ACR. What is the simplest fix?",["Add a ConfigMap with the registry URL","Increase the pod memory limit","Change the Service type to LoadBalancer","Run az aks update --attach-acr"],3,"--attach-acr gives the cluster's kubelet identity the AcrPull role on the registry. ConfigMaps, memory limits and Service types do not affect image pulls.","Skills 1: AKS",["A ConfigMap only passes configuration to pods; the registry URL is already in the image name and the problem is missing pull permission.","Memory limits affect running containers, but ErrImagePull happens before the container starts, when the image cannot be pulled.","The Service type controls how traffic reaches pods; it has no effect on whether nodes can pull the image.",null],2],["az12",0,1,"A Kubernetes Deployment manifest should keep three copies of an API pod running. Which field sets this?",["spec.replicas","spec.template.spec.containers.count","metadata.labels.replicas","spec.strategy.maxSurge"],0,"spec.replicas sets the desired number of pods. maxSurge only controls how many extra pods may be created during a rolling update.","Skills 1: AKS manifests",[null,"containers is a list of container definitions in the pod template; there is no count field and it does not control how many pods run.","Labels are key/value metadata used for selection; a label named replicas has no effect on the pod count.","maxSurge limits how many extra pods can be created during a rolling update; it does not set the steady-state count."],1],["az13",0,1,"A Python image is 1.2 GB because it includes compilers used only to build wheels. How can the developer shrink the final image?",["Add more layers with RUN rm commands at the end","Use a multi-stage build with a slim final image","Change the tag from latest to slim","Enable ACR geo-replication"],1,"A multi-stage build keeps build tools in an earlier stage and copies only the output into a small final image. Deleting files in a later layer does not shrink earlier layers.","Skills 1: Dockerfiles",["Files deleted in a later layer still exist in the earlier layers, so the image does not get smaller.",null,"Changing the tag to slim only changes the base image; the compilers installed during the build would still end up in the image.","Geo-replication copies the image to more regions; it does not change the image size and adds storage."],1],["az14",0,1,"Which statement about Container Apps secrets is correct?",["Secrets are stored in the container image and read at build time","Secrets can only be plain text typed into the portal","Secrets are shared automatically with every app in the environment","Secrets belong to one app and can reference Key Vault"],3,"Container Apps secrets belong to one app and can be Key Vault references resolved with a managed identity. They are not baked into images or shared across apps.","Skills 1: Container Apps secrets",["Container Apps secrets are app-level settings injected at runtime; they are never baked into the container image.","Secrets can be plain values or Key Vault references resolved with a managed identity, so they are not limited to portal-typed text.","Secrets are scoped to a single Container App and are not shared automatically with other apps in the environment.",null],2],["az15",0,1,"A web app on App Service runs a custom container. The team wants to test a new image in production conditions and swap it live with no downtime. What should they use?",["A deployment slot, then swap","A second App Service plan","Scale out to two instances","A new ACR webhook on the production slot"],0,"Deployment slots let you deploy and warm up a new version, then swap it with production. Scaling out or adding a plan does not give a staged swap.","Skills 1: App Service containers",[null,"A second App Service plan is just more compute; it has no built-in staging or swap between versions.","Scaling out adds instances of the same version; it does not let you stage and swap a new image.","An ACR webhook can trigger continuous deployment, but on the production slot it would push the new image live without a staged test and swap."],1],["az16",0,1,"Which statement correctly describes Azure Container Apps environments?",["Each app must be in its own environment","Environments are Kubernetes clusters you manage with kubectl","Apps in an environment share networking and logging","Environments exist only for Container Apps jobs"],2,"An environment is a secure boundary around a group of apps that share networking and Log Analytics settings. Microsoft manages the underlying Kubernetes; you do not use kubectl on it.","Skills 1: Container Apps",["Many apps can share one environment; putting each app in its own environment is optional, not required.","The underlying Kubernetes is managed by Microsoft and hidden; you cannot use kubectl against a Container Apps environment.",null,"Environments host both Container Apps and Container Apps jobs; they are not limited to jobs."],1],["az17",0,1,"An ACR repository holds thousands of old image versions, and storage costs keep rising. What should the developer set up?",["Geo-replication to spread storage across regions","A scheduled acr purge task with an age filter","Content trust on the repository","A new registry for each release"],1,"acr purge in a scheduled task deletes tags and manifests older than a set age that match a filter. Geo-replication adds copies and raises storage.","Skills 1: ACR",["Geo-replication adds copies of every image to more regions, which increases storage instead of reducing it.",null,"Content trust signs images so clients can verify them; it does not delete old versions or reduce storage.","A new registry per release multiplies registries to manage and still keeps every old image unless something deletes them."],1],["az18",0,1,"In a Kubernetes Deployment, what is the effect of setting resources.requests for CPU and memory?",["The scheduler places the pod only on a node with that much unreserved capacity","The container is killed as soon as its usage reaches that amount of CPU or memory","The pod gets a public IP address","The image is pulled only once per node"],0,"Requests are what the scheduler reserves when placing a pod. Limits, not requests, cap usage (a container over its memory limit is killed).","Skills 1: AKS manifests",[null,"Killing a container over its memory usage is the effect of limits, not requests; CPU over a limit is throttled rather than killed.","Public IPs come from Services such as LoadBalancer, not from resource requests on the pod.","Image pull behavior is controlled by imagePullPolicy and node caching, not by resource requests."],3],["az19",0,1,"A developer wants to run a new Container App revision for testing through its own URL without sending it any production traffic. What can they use?",["An internal ingress on a second environment","A KEDA rule with min replicas 0","Single revision mode with a new suffix","A revision label with 0% traffic weight"],3,"In multiple revision mode, a label gives a revision its own stable URL, and a 0% weight keeps it out of the main traffic split. Single revision mode would move all traffic to it.","Skills 1: Container Apps revisions",["A second environment would be a separate deployment with its own network, not a test URL for one revision of the same app.","A KEDA rule with min replicas 0 only controls scaling; it gives the revision no separate URL.","In single revision mode the new revision receives all traffic as soon as it is ready, which is the opposite of the goal.",null],2],["az20",0,1,"Why should a container run as a non-root user?",["It makes the image build faster","It is required for ACR to accept the push","It limits damage if an attacker breaks into the process","It lets the container listen on port 80 without extra settings"],2,"A non-root user reduces what an attacker can do after compromising the app. It is not required by ACR, and binding to ports below 1024 is actually harder without root.","Skills 1: Dockerfiles",["The user a container runs as has no effect on how quickly the image layers are built.","ACR accepts images regardless of the user they run as; running as non-root is a runtime hardening practice, not a push requirement.",null,"It is the opposite: binding to ports below 1024 normally needs root or an extra capability, so non-root apps usually listen on a high port."],1],["az21",0,1,"A team deploys the same image to Container Apps in three regions and wants pulls to stay in-region for speed. What should they use?",["Three Basic registries kept in sync with a nightly copy script","A Premium registry with geo-replication to each region","A Standard registry with a CDN in front","An ACR token per region"],1,"Geo-replication on Premium keeps one registry name while serving pulls from a nearby replica. Separate registries need manual syncing, and ACR does not sit behind a CDN.","Skills 1: ACR",["Separate Basic registries need custom sync scripts, have different names per region and can drift out of date.",null,"ACR cannot be placed behind a CDN for image pulls, and Standard has no geo-replication.","ACR tokens are scoped credentials for repository access; they do not change where images are served from."],2],["az22",0,2,"An app reads a user profile by id many times per second, and the container is partitioned by /userId. Which approach costs the fewest RUs?",["A query with SELECT * FROM c WHERE c.id = @id AND c.userId = @uid","A cross-partition query with ORDER BY","A point read using read_item(item=id, partition_key=userId)","A change feed read filtered by id"],2,"A point read with the id and partition key is the cheapest operation, about 1 RU for a 1 KB item. A query for the same item costs more because the engine must process it.","Skills 2: Cosmos DB SDK",["A query for one item still goes through the query engine and costs more RUs than a point read, even with the partition key.","A cross-partition query with ORDER BY fans out to every partition and is one of the most expensive ways to fetch a single item.",null,"The change feed lists changes over time; it is not a lookup mechanism and reading it to find one item wastes RUs."],2],["az23",0,2,"A multi-tenant SaaS app stores documents in Cosmos DB. Most queries filter by tenantId, and some tenants are far larger than others. Which partition key choice avoids hot partitions while keeping queries targeted?",["A hierarchical partition key such as /tenantId then /userId","/country, because it has few values","/createdDate, because it grows over time","/type, because it cleanly separates the different kinds of documents"],0,"A hierarchical key keeps tenant queries targeted while spreading large tenants across more partitions. Low-cardinality or date keys concentrate writes on a few partitions.","Skills 2: Partitioning",[null,"/country has very few distinct values, so a few large countries would create hot partitions.","A date key sends all of today's writes to the same partition, creating a hot partition for writes.","/type has only a handful of values, so partitions would be huge and uneven and tenant queries would fan out."],3],["az24",0,2,"Each item in a Cosmos DB container stores a 1,536-dimension embedding at /embedding. Write RU cost is high. What should the developer change?",["Switch the account to Strong consistency","Exclude /embedding/* from the range index and use a vector index for that path","Remove the partition key","Store the embedding as a string instead"],1,"Indexing every number of an embedding in the normal index wastes RUs; the path should be excluded and covered by a vector index instead. Strong consistency adds cost rather than reducing it.","Skills 2: Cosmos DB vector search",["Strong consistency changes read guarantees and raises read cost; it does nothing to reduce indexing cost on writes.",null,"Every container must have a partition key; removing it is not possible and would not reduce indexing cost.","Storing the embedding as a string would break vector search and still gets indexed by the range index."],3],["az25",0,2,"Which Cosmos DB for NoSQL query returns the five items most similar to a query vector @v?",["SELECT TOP 5 c.id FROM c ORDER BY VectorDistance(c.embedding, @v)","SELECT c.id FROM c WHERE c.embedding = @v LIMIT 5","SELECT TOP 5 c.id FROM c WHERE ARRAY_CONTAINS(c.embedding, @v, true)","SELECT TOP 5 c.id FROM c ORDER BY c.embedding DESC"],0,"VectorDistance computes similarity using the container's vector policy, and ORDER BY with TOP returns the nearest items. Equality, ARRAY_CONTAINS and plain ORDER BY do not compare vectors by similarity.","Skills 2: Cosmos DB vector search",[null,"Equality compares the whole array for an exact match; it does not measure similarity, and Cosmos DB NoSQL uses TOP rather than LIMIT.","ARRAY_CONTAINS checks whether an array contains a value; it does not rank items by vector similarity.","ORDER BY on the embedding array sorts by array values, not by distance to the query vector @v."],2],["az26",0,2,"A developer creates a new container for embeddings. Which setting must be defined in the container vector policy?",["The consistency level","The throughput in RU/s","The TTL for each vector","The embedding path, data type, dimensions and distance function"],3,"The vector policy describes each embedding path's data type, dimensions and distance function. Consistency is set on the account, and throughput and TTL are separate container settings.","Skills 2: Cosmos DB vector search",["Consistency is an account-level setting (optionally relaxed per request), not part of the container vector policy.","Throughput is provisioned on the database or container separately and is not part of the vector policy.","TTL is set on the container or per item for the whole document; vectors have no separate TTL in the policy.",null],1],["az27",0,2,"Which Cosmos DB vector index type is designed for large collections and gives fast approximate search with low latency at scale?",["flat","range","diskANN","spatial"],2,"diskANN is the approximate nearest neighbor index built for large vector sets. flat does exact search and suits small sets; range and spatial are not vector indexes.","Skills 2: Cosmos DB vector search",["flat performs exact brute-force search and is limited to 505 dimensions, so it suits small sets rather than large collections.","A range index supports equality and range filters on scalar values; it is not a vector index.",null,"A spatial index supports geospatial queries on GeoJSON points and polygons, not vector similarity search."],1],["az28",0,2,"New product descriptions are written to Cosmos DB. An embedding must be generated for each new or updated item without polling. What should the developer use?",["A timer trigger that queries all items for changes once every minute","An Azure Functions Cosmos DB trigger based on the change feed","A Cosmos DB stored procedure called by the client","Strong consistency with a read-after-write"],1,"The Cosmos DB trigger reads the change feed and runs for each batch of new or changed items. A timer that queries everything is polling and costs far more RUs.","Skills 2: Change feed",["A timer that queries everything each minute is polling, adds latency and burns RUs scanning unchanged items.",null,"Stored procedures run only when a client calls them; they cannot react automatically to new or changed items.","Consistency and read-after-write affect what a client reads, not whether anything runs when data changes."],2],["az29",0,2,"A change feed processor runs on three instances. How do the instances avoid processing the same changes twice?",["Each instance reads a different database","They use Strong consistency","Each instance filters by a random number","They share a lease container that tracks which instance owns each partition range"],3,"The lease container stores ownership and checkpoints for each feed range so work is split and progress survives restarts. Consistency level does not coordinate consumers.","Skills 2: Change feed",["Instances of one processor read the same monitored container; splitting by database is not how work is shared.","Consistency level affects read guarantees; it does not coordinate which consumer handles which partition range.","Random filtering would drop or duplicate changes and gives no checkpointing across restarts.",null],3],["az30",0,2,"A developer notices that deleted items never show up in the change feed in latest version mode. What is a common way to handle deletes?",["Use a soft-delete flag and let TTL remove the item later","Switch to Eventual consistency","Read the change feed from the beginning each time","Use a stored procedure that raises an error whenever an item is deleted"],0,"Latest version mode does not include deletes, so a soft-delete field (an update the feed sees) plus TTL is a standard pattern. All versions and deletes mode is the other option.","Skills 2: Change feed",[null,"Consistency level does not control what the change feed contains; latest version mode omits deletes at every level.","Re-reading from the beginning still shows only current items, so deleted items remain invisible and RU cost rises.","Stored procedures do not run on deletes made through other paths, and raising an error would block deletes rather than capture them."],3],["az31",0,2,"Which Cosmos DB consistency level is the default and gives read-your-own-writes within a client session at lower cost than Strong?",["Eventual","Session","Consistent prefix","Bounded staleness"],1,"Session consistency is the default and guarantees a client sees its own writes. Strong and bounded staleness cost more RUs for reads; eventual and consistent prefix give weaker guarantees.","Skills 2: Consistency",["Eventual is the weakest level and does not guarantee a client sees its own writes.",null,"Consistent prefix guarantees ordering of writes but not read-your-own-writes, and it is not the default.","Bounded staleness is not the default and costs more for reads than Session; it bounds lag by time or versions."],1],["az32",0,2,"A query in the Python SDK filters on a user-supplied category. How should the value be passed?",["Build the SQL string with an f-string","Put the value in the partition key","Use parameters=[{'name': '@cat', 'value': category}] with WHERE c.category = @cat","Escape quotes manually before concatenating"],2,"Parameterized queries prevent injection and let the service reuse the query plan. String building with f-strings or manual escaping is unsafe.","Skills 2: Cosmos DB SDK",["An f-string builds the SQL from user input, which allows query injection and prevents plan reuse.","The partition key routes the query to a partition; putting the user's category there is not how you pass a filter value.",null,"Manual escaping is error-prone and still concatenates user input into the query text."],2],["az33",0,2,"On Azure Database for PostgreSQL flexible server, CREATE EXTENSION vector fails with a message that the extension is not allowed. What must the developer do first?",["Upgrade to the Memory Optimized tier","Install the pgvector package with pip on the client machine first","Enable pgBouncer","Add VECTOR to the azure.extensions server parameter"],3,"Flexible server only allows extensions listed in the azure.extensions server parameter. pgvector is a server extension, not a client package, and the tier does not matter.","Skills 2: PostgreSQL pgvector",["The service tier does not decide which extensions are allowed; the allow-list parameter does.","pgvector is a server-side extension; installing a Python client package does not make CREATE EXTENSION work on the server.","pgBouncer is a connection pooler; enabling it has nothing to do with extension allow-listing.",null],2],["az34",0,2,"In pgvector, which operator returns cosine distance between two vectors?",["<=>","<->","<#>","@@"],0,"<=> is cosine distance, <-> is Euclidean (L2) distance and <#> is negative inner product. @@ is PostgreSQL full-text match.","Skills 2: PostgreSQL pgvector",[null,"<-> is Euclidean (L2) distance, not cosine distance.","<#> returns the negative inner product, not cosine distance.","@@ is the PostgreSQL full-text search match operator and does not work on vectors."],1],["az35",0,2,"A table of 2 million document chunks needs fast approximate nearest neighbor search with high recall, and rows keep arriving. Which pgvector index is the better fit?",["A B-tree index on the embedding column","HNSW with vector_cosine_ops","IVFFlat created on the empty table before loading","A GIN index on the text column"],1,"HNSW gives high recall and fast queries and can be built on an empty table or as data arrives. IVFFlat should be built after data is loaded so its lists reflect the data; B-tree and GIN do not index vector similarity.","Skills 2: PostgreSQL pgvector",["B-tree indexes support ordering and equality on scalar values; they cannot accelerate vector similarity search.",null,"IVFFlat built on an empty table has no data to form its lists, so recall is poor; it should be built after loading.","A GIN index on the text column helps full-text or JSON search, not nearest neighbor search on embeddings."],3],["az36",0,2,"An HNSW index returns fast results, but some relevant chunks are missing. Which setting can raise recall at query time?",["Lower ivfflat.probes","Reduce the vector dimensions","Increase hnsw.ef_search","Drop and recreate the index with m = 2"],2,"hnsw.ef_search sets how many candidates the search keeps, so a higher value improves recall at some speed cost. probes applies to IVFFlat, and a tiny m lowers recall.","Skills 2: PostgreSQL pgvector",["ivfflat.probes applies only to IVFFlat indexes, and lowering it would reduce recall rather than raise it.","Changing the dimensions would require re-embedding every row with a different model and does not tune the index.",null,"m = 2 builds a very sparse graph, which lowers recall; it is also a build-time setting, not a query-time one."],3],["az37",0,2,"A RAG app must only return chunks that belong to the signed-in customer. How should retrieval be written in PostgreSQL?",["Filter results in the prompt by asking the model to ignore other customers","Store each customer's chunks in a separate embedding model","Sort by customer_id before the vector search","Add WHERE customer_id = %s to the vector similarity query"],3,"Metadata filtering in the query guarantees that only allowed rows are retrieved. Asking the model to ignore data is not a security control.","Skills 2: RAG",["Instructions in the prompt are not a security boundary; other customers' data would already have been retrieved and exposed to the model.","Using a separate embedding model per customer is unnecessary and does not filter rows in the database.","Sorting by customer_id only reorders rows; it does not exclude other customers' chunks from the results.",null],3],["az38",0,2,"What is the correct order of steps when ingesting documents for RAG?",["Chunk the text, create embeddings, store text, embeddings and metadata","Create one embedding for the whole library, then chunk it","Send whole documents to the model, then store the answers","Store the full text first, then embed each user question and save it in the table"],0,"Documents are split into chunks, each chunk is embedded, and text, vectors and metadata are stored for retrieval. Questions are embedded at query time, not stored as the corpus.","Skills 2: RAG",[null,"One embedding for the whole library blurs all content into one vector, making precise retrieval impossible.","Storing model answers to whole documents is not ingestion for retrieval and skips chunking and embeddings.","User questions are embedded at query time; storing them in the table does not build the searchable corpus."],1],["az39",0,2,"A product catalog API reads from PostgreSQL on every request, and read latency is too high. Prices change a few times a day. What should the developer implement with Azure Managed Redis?",["Write-behind caching of all database writes","Cache-aside with a TTL and deletion of the key when a price changes","Store the catalog only in Redis with no TTL","Use Redis as the primary database for the catalog with persistence turned off"],1,"Cache-aside reads the cache first, fills it on a miss, and uses a TTL plus invalidation on change to keep data fresh. Making Redis the only store risks data loss.","Skills 2: Redis caching",["Write-behind caches database writes and flushes them later; it adds risk and does not address read latency here.",null,"With no TTL and Redis as the only store, price changes are never refreshed and data can be lost.","Using Redis as the primary database with persistence off means a restart loses the catalog."],2],["az40",0,2,"A chatbot sends many questions that are worded differently but mean the same thing, and model costs are high. Which Redis-based technique reduces repeated model calls?",["Key expiration on session tokens","Pub/Sub channels for each user","A semantic cache that stores responses keyed by the question's embedding","A sorted set of question lengths"],2,"A semantic cache looks up earlier answers whose question embeddings are close to the new one, so similar questions reuse a response. The other features do not match by meaning.","Skills 2: Redis vector search",["Expiring session tokens manages logins; it does nothing to reuse answers for similar questions.","Pub/Sub delivers messages to subscribers in real time; it stores nothing and cannot match questions by meaning.",null,"Question length says nothing about meaning, so sorting by length cannot find semantically similar questions."],2],["az41",0,2,"Redis memory is full, and the cache should drop the least recently used keys that have a TTL while keeping keys without one. Which eviction policy fits?",["volatile-lru","allkeys-random","noeviction","allkeys-lfu"],0,"volatile-lru evicts least recently used keys among those with an expiry. allkeys policies can evict any key, and noeviction returns errors on writes when memory is full.","Skills 2: Redis caching",[null,"allkeys-random evicts any key at random, including keys without a TTL that must be kept.","noeviction evicts nothing and returns errors on writes when memory is full.","allkeys-lfu can evict any key, including those without a TTL, and uses frequency rather than recency."],2],["az42",0,2,"A team's documents are already in relational tables with foreign keys and they need RAG with joins and filters on those tables. Which store is the best fit for the vectors?",["Azure Managed Redis as the only store","A new Cosmos DB account with the documents copied","Azure Blob Storage with index tags","Azure Database for PostgreSQL with pgvector"],3,"pgvector keeps vectors next to the relational data so SQL joins and filters work in one query. Copying to another store adds sync work, and Redis is best as a cache in front of a source of truth.","Skills 2: Choosing a store",["Redis is a fast cache but is not a good fit as the only store for relational data needing joins and foreign keys.","Copying relational data into Cosmos DB adds a sync pipeline and loses SQL joins across the existing tables.","Blob index tags are simple key/value filters on blobs; Blob Storage has no vector search or relational joins.",null],3],["az43",0,2,"A Python app gets HTTP 429 responses from Cosmos DB during bursts. What does this mean, and what is a good first response?",["The partition key is missing; recreate the container","Provisioned RU/s were exceeded; let the SDK retry after the given delay and review RU use or autoscale","The consistency level is too weak; switch to Strong","The item is larger than 2 MB; split the item"],1,"429 means the request rate is too large for the provisioned throughput. The SDK retries using the retry-after header; persistent 429s call for query tuning or more (autoscale) RU/s.","Skills 2: Request Units",["A missing partition key would cause a different error at write time; 429 means the request rate was too high.",null,"Consistency has nothing to do with 429s; Strong would actually increase RU cost for reads.","Items over 2 MB fail with a 413 (request entity too large), not a 429."],2],["az44",0,2,"Which Cosmos DB indexing policy change lowers write RU cost for items with many properties that are never queried?",["Switch to Strong consistency","Add a composite index on every property","Keep the default policy that automatically indexes every path","Include only the queried paths and exclude the rest"],3,"Indexing fewer paths means less index maintenance on every write. The default indexes everything, and composite indexes add cost.","Skills 2: Request Units",["Consistency affects read cost and guarantees; it does not reduce index maintenance on writes.","Composite indexes add more index entries on every write, raising write RU cost.","Indexing every path by default is what makes writes expensive here.",null],1],["az45",0,2,"When is Azure Managed Redis a poor choice as the place to store embeddings?",["When vectors need millisecond lookups","When the vectors are only a cache in front of another durable store","When it would be the only durable copy of business-critical data","When results can expire after an hour"],2,"Redis is an in-memory service best used as a fast cache or index; the durable source of truth should live in a database. Fast lookups and expiring data are good Redis fits.","Skills 2: Choosing a store",["Millisecond lookups are exactly what an in-memory store like Redis is good at, so this is a good fit, not a poor one.","Using Redis as a cache of vectors backed by another durable store is a recommended pattern.",null,"Data that may expire after an hour suits Redis TTLs well, so this is a good fit."],3],["az46",0,2,"A developer builds an IVFFlat index and sees poor recall. Which change is most likely to help?",["Rebuild the index after loading the data and raise ivfflat.probes","Lower hnsw.ef_search","Switch the operator from <=> to @@","Decrease the lists value to 1 and set probes to 0"],0,"IVFFlat learns its lists from existing data, so building it on a full table and searching more lists (probes) improves recall. ef_search is an HNSW setting and @@ is full-text search.","Skills 2: PostgreSQL pgvector",[null,"hnsw.ef_search only affects HNSW indexes, and lowering it would reduce recall anyway.","@@ is the full-text search operator and cannot compare vectors; switching operators would break the query.","One list and zero probes would make the index useless; probes must be at least 1 and more probes, not fewer, raise recall."],3],["az47",0,3,"An order service publishes each order once, and the billing, shipping and email services must each get their own copy. Which Service Bus setup fits?",["One queue with three competing receivers","A topic with three subscriptions","Three queues on the Basic tier with auto-forwarding","One queue with sessions enabled"],1,"A topic delivers a copy of each message to every subscription. Competing receivers on one queue split messages so each message goes to only one of them.","Skills 3: Service Bus",["Competing receivers on one queue split the messages, so each order would reach only one of the three services.",null,"Basic tier has no topics and auto-forwarding chains entities; the sender would have to send to three queues itself.","Sessions group related messages for ordered processing by one receiver; they do not copy a message to several services."],1],["az48",0,3,"A receiver uses peek-lock. Processing fails on a message that is malformed and will never succeed. What should the code do?",["Call abandon_message so it can be retried forever","Call complete_message so it is removed silently","Call dead_letter_message with a reason","Let the lock expire"],2,"Dead-lettering a poison message moves it aside with a reason for inspection. Abandon or lock expiry only retry it, and complete loses it without a record.","Skills 3: Service Bus",["Abandoning returns the message to the queue, so a message that can never succeed is retried until the max delivery count wastes processing.","Completing removes the message with no record, so the bad data is lost and cannot be inspected.",null,"Letting the lock expire just redelivers the message and increases the delivery count, delaying the inevitable."],2],["az49",0,3,"Messages for the same customer must be processed in order, but different customers can be processed in parallel. Which feature should be used?",["Duplicate detection","Scheduled messages","Auto-forwarding","Sessions with SessionId set to the customer id"],3,"Sessions group messages by SessionId, and one receiver locks a session at a time, which keeps each customer's messages ordered while other sessions run in parallel.","Skills 3: Service Bus",["Duplicate detection drops repeated MessageIds within a time window; it does not order messages per customer.","Scheduled messages set a future enqueue time; they do not guarantee per-customer ordering.","Auto-forwarding moves messages from one entity to another; it has no ordering or grouping semantics.",null],2],["az50",0,3,"A message keeps returning to the queue after failed attempts. After how many deliveries does Service Bus move it to the dead-letter queue by default?",["10","3","5","30"],0,"The default max delivery count is 10. After that the message is moved to the dead-letter subqueue.","Skills 3: Service Bus",[null,"3 is a common custom setting, but the default max delivery count is 10.","5 is not the default; Service Bus uses 10 unless you change it.","30 is Event Grid's default max delivery attempts, not Service Bus's max delivery count."],1],["az51",0,3,"A client may resend the same order message after a network timeout. How can Service Bus drop the duplicate automatically?",["Enable sessions on the queue","Enable duplicate detection and set a stable MessageId","Lower the lock duration","Use receive-and-delete mode"],1,"Duplicate detection discards messages with a MessageId already seen in the detection window. Sessions and receive modes do not remove duplicates.","Skills 3: Service Bus",["Sessions group and order messages by SessionId; a resent message would still be delivered twice.",null,"Lock duration affects how long a receiver holds a message; it does nothing about duplicate sends.","Receive-and-delete changes how messages are consumed and can lose messages; it does not detect duplicates."],2],["az52",0,3,"A subscription should get only messages where the application property priority equals 'high'. What should be added?",["A second topic just for the high-priority messages from the sender","A session on the subscription","A subscription rule with a SQL filter priority = 'high'","Auto-forwarding from the topic"],2,"Subscription rules with SQL or correlation filters choose which messages a subscription receives. A second topic would require changing the sender.","Skills 3: Service Bus",["A separate topic forces the sender to choose where to publish, instead of letting the subscription filter messages.","Sessions provide ordered grouping by SessionId; they do not filter on application properties.",null,"Auto-forwarding moves messages between entities; it does not select messages by property."],2],["az53",0,3,"A startup wants the cheapest Service Bus tier but needs publish-subscribe with topics. Which tier is the minimum?",["Basic","Premium","Standard","Any tier with partitioning enabled"],2,"Topics need at least Standard; Basic supports only queues. Premium works but is not the minimum.","Skills 3: Service Bus",["Basic supports queues only; it has no topics or subscriptions.","Premium supports topics but costs more; it is not the minimum tier that does.",null,"Partitioning is a scale feature and does not add topics to the Basic tier."],1],["az54",0,3,"A function must run when a blob is created in a storage account, and the team wants low-latency push delivery instead of polling. Which approach is recommended?",["A timer trigger that lists new blobs","A queue trigger reading storage logs","An HTTP trigger that the upload page calls from the browser","A blob trigger that uses an Event Grid source"],3,"The Event Grid-based blob trigger receives push notifications for new blobs with low latency. Timers and log reading are polling.","Skills 3: Azure Functions",["A timer that lists blobs is polling; it adds delay and cost as the container grows.","Reading storage logs through a queue is an indirect, delayed approach and not the recommended trigger.","Relying on the upload page misses blobs created by any other path and puts trust in client-side code.",null],2],["az55",0,3,"An Event Grid subscription should deliver only BlobCreated events for files ending in .pdf. What should be configured?",["An event type filter plus a subject ends-with filter of .pdf","A dead-letter destination","A retry policy with 1 attempt","A CloudEvents schema on the topic"],0,"Event type and subject filters on the subscription limit what is delivered. Dead-lettering, retries and schema do not filter events.","Skills 3: Event Grid",[null,"A dead-letter destination stores events that could not be delivered; it does not filter what is sent.","The retry policy controls delivery attempts after failure and does not filter events by type or name.","The event schema only changes the envelope format; it does not filter events."],2],["az56",0,3,"A webhook subscriber was down for two days. Events from that time were lost. What should have been configured to keep them?",["A shorter event time-to-live","Dead-lettering to a Blob Storage container","A second custom topic","The Event Grid event schema instead of CloudEvents"],1,"When retries run out, Event Grid writes undelivered events to the dead-letter storage container if one is set. Without it they are dropped.","Skills 3: Event Grid",["A shorter time-to-live makes Event Grid give up sooner, which would lose events faster.",null,"A second topic does not help because the undelivered events on the original subscription would still be dropped.","The schema only changes the event format; neither schema keeps undelivered events."],2],["az57",0,3,"Which Event Grid behavior is correct for a failed delivery to a webhook?",["It retries once, then deletes the event","It retries with backoff until max attempts or the TTL expires","It stores the event in a Service Bus queue","It calls the webhook every second until the endpoint finally succeeds"],1,"Event Grid retries on a backoff schedule, bounded by max delivery attempts and event time-to-live (30 and 24 hours by default).","Skills 3: Event Grid",["Event Grid retries many times on a backoff schedule, not once; events are dropped or dead-lettered only when limits are reached.",null,"Event Grid does not store failed events in Service Bus; it dead-letters to a Blob Storage container if configured.","Event Grid uses exponential backoff, not a fixed one-second retry forever, and gives up at max attempts or TTL."],2],["az58",0,3,"A telemetry pipeline ingests millions of device readings per second and must let several consumers replay the stream. Which service fits best?",["Event Grid custom topic","Service Bus queue","Azure Event Hubs","Azure Queue Storage"],2,"Event Hubs is a partitioned stream built for high-volume telemetry with replay by offset. Service Bus is for business messages, and Event Grid is for discrete events.","Skills 3: Choosing a service",["Event Grid pushes discrete events and has no retention or replay by offset for high-volume streams.","Service Bus queues are for business messages; they cannot sustain millions of events per second or let several consumers replay the stream.",null,"Queue Storage is a simple queue with competing consumers and no replay, far below the needed throughput."],1],["az59",0,3,"A Python function should run every 15 minutes. Which schedule expression is correct for a timer trigger?",["*/15 * * * *","0 */15 * * * *","15 * * * * *","0 0 */15 * * *"],1,"Functions timer triggers use six-field NCRONTAB with seconds first: 0 */15 * * * * means second 0 of every 15th minute. The five-field form is missing seconds, and the last option runs every 15 hours.","Skills 3: Azure Functions",["This is five-field cron without seconds; Functions NCRONTAB needs six fields, so it is rejected.",null,"This fires at second 15 of every minute, which is once a minute, not every 15 minutes.","*/15 in the hour field fires at hours 0 and 15, not every 15 minutes."],2],["az60",0,3,"In the Python v2 programming model, where are triggers and bindings declared?",["In a function.json file next to each function","In host.json under extensions","In local.settings.json","As decorators on functions in function_app.py"],3,"The v2 model uses decorators on a FunctionApp object in function_app.py. function.json belongs to the v1 model, and host.json holds app-wide runtime settings.","Skills 3: Azure Functions",["function.json files belong to the Python v1 programming model, not v2.","host.json holds app-wide runtime and extension settings, not per-function triggers and bindings.","local.settings.json stores app settings for local runs, not trigger and binding definitions.",null],1],["az61",0,3,"A Service Bus-triggered function must connect without a connection string. Which app setting shape enables an identity-based connection named ServiceBusConn?",["ServiceBusConn__fullyQualifiedNamespace = mynamespace.servicebus.windows.net","ServiceBusConn = Endpoint=sb://...;SharedAccessKey=...","AzureWebJobsServiceBus = managedidentity","ServiceBusConn__clientSecret = <secret>"],0,"The __fullyQualifiedNamespace suffix tells the extension to use an identity, and the identity needs a Service Bus data role. The second option is a key-based connection string.","Skills 3: Azure Functions",[null,"A connection string with SharedAccessKey is key-based authentication, which is what the team wants to avoid.","AzureWebJobsServiceBus = managedidentity is not a valid setting shape; identity-based connections use the __fullyQualifiedNamespace suffix.","A client secret is a stored credential; identity-based connections with a managed identity need none."],3],["az62",0,3,"An HTTP-triggered function should require a key from callers but not an admin master key. Which auth level fits?",["anonymous","admin","function","system"],2,"The function level requires a function or host key. anonymous needs no key and admin needs the master key.","Skills 3: Azure Functions",["anonymous lets anyone call the function without any key.","admin requires the master key, which the requirement explicitly excludes.",null,"system is a key type used by extensions such as webhooks, not the auth level for callers of an HTTP trigger."],1],["az63",0,3,"A function app must scale to zero when idle but also reach private resources through a virtual network. Which hosting plan is designed for this?",["Flex Consumption","Dedicated on a Basic App Service plan","Premium with zero always-ready instances","Container Apps with min replicas 1"],0,"Flex Consumption supports scale to zero and virtual network integration. Premium keeps at least one instance warm, and Dedicated plans do not scale to zero.","Skills 3: Azure Functions hosting",[null,"Dedicated (App Service) plans run instances continuously and do not scale to zero.","Premium always keeps at least one ready instance, so it cannot scale to zero.","Container Apps is not a Functions hosting plan here, and min replicas 1 keeps a replica running."],3],["az64",0,3,"A function should read an order from a Service Bus queue and write a document to Cosmos DB with no SDK code for the write. What should the developer use?",["A timer trigger and a Cosmos DB input binding","A Service Bus trigger and a Cosmos DB output binding","An HTTP trigger and a Service Bus output binding for the document","A Cosmos DB trigger and a queue input binding"],1,"The Service Bus trigger starts the function, and a Cosmos DB output binding writes the document. The other pairs have the wrong trigger or direction.","Skills 3: Azure Functions bindings",["A timer trigger runs on a schedule rather than when an order arrives, and an input binding reads rather than writes.",null,"An HTTP trigger starts on web requests, and a Service Bus output binding sends messages rather than writing a Cosmos DB document.","A Cosmos DB trigger fires on Cosmos DB changes, not on queue messages, and there is no Service Bus queue input binding."],2],["az65",0,3,"A developer runs a function app locally. Where should connection settings for local runs be stored?",["host.json","requirements.txt","local.settings.json (kept out of source control)","function_app.py as constants"],2,"local.settings.json holds app settings for local runs and should not be committed. In Azure, the same names are app settings on the function app.","Skills 3: Azure Functions",["host.json holds runtime configuration that is deployed with the app; it is not for connection secrets.","requirements.txt lists Python packages to install, not app settings.",null,"Hard-coding connection details in function_app.py puts secrets in source control and needs code changes per environment."],1],["az66",0,3,"A Service Bus receiver takes several minutes per message and sometimes loses its lock, so the message is delivered twice. What should the developer do?",["Switch to the Basic tier","Enable duplicate detection on the queue with a ten-minute history window","Lower the max delivery count to 1","Renew the message lock during processing or use automatic lock renewal"],3,"Renewing the lock (the SDK's AutoLockRenewer in Python) keeps long processing from losing the message. Duplicate detection works on sends, not on redelivery.","Skills 3: Service Bus",["Basic tier has the same lock behavior and removes features; it does not fix lost locks.","Duplicate detection filters duplicate sends by MessageId; it does not stop redelivery after a lost lock.","A max delivery count of 1 would dead-letter messages after one lost lock instead of processing them.",null],3],["az67",0,3,"A webhook endpoint is added as an Event Grid subscriber, but the subscription stays in a failed state. What is the most likely cause?",["The topic uses the CloudEvents schema instead of the Event Grid schema","The endpoint did not complete the subscription validation handshake","The subscription has no subject filter","Dead-lettering is disabled"],1,"Event Grid validates webhook endpoints before sending events; the endpoint must echo the validation code (or open the validation URL). Filters and dead-lettering are optional.","Skills 3: Event Grid",["Event Grid supports CloudEvents for webhooks, so the schema choice alone does not put a subscription in a failed state.",null,"Subject filters are optional; a subscription with no filter receives all events and is valid.","Dead-lettering is optional and affects only undelivered events, not subscription creation."],3],["az68",0,4,"An app on Container Apps must read secrets from Key Vault. The same Python code must run on developer laptops with az login. Which credential class should it use?",["ClientSecretCredential with a stored secret","DefaultAzureCredential","UsernamePasswordCredential","AzureNamedKeyCredential"],1,"DefaultAzureCredential tries managed identity in Azure and developer sign-ins such as the Azure CLI locally, so the code does not change. The others need stored secrets or keys.","Skills 4: Managed identity",["ClientSecretCredential needs a stored client secret, which defeats managed identity and must be rotated.",null,"UsernamePasswordCredential uses a user's password, does not work with MFA and is not for service workloads.","AzureNamedKeyCredential wraps a shared account key for some services; it does not use Entra ID identities."],2],["az69",0,4,"Five container apps must use one identity that is granted access to Key Vault before any of them is deployed. What should be used?",["A system-assigned identity on each app","A shared storage account key","A user-assigned managed identity","The Key Vault access policy for All Users"],2,"A user-assigned identity is created on its own, can be granted roles in advance and shared by several apps. System-assigned identities exist only once each app is created.","Skills 4: Managed identity",["A system-assigned identity exists only after its app is created, so it cannot be granted access before deployment and is not shared.","A storage account key is unrelated to Key Vault access and is a shared secret, not an identity.",null,"Access policies have no All Users option, and granting everyone access would violate least privilege."],2],["az70",0,4,"Using the Azure RBAC permission model, which role should an app's identity get to read secret values from Key Vault with least privilege?",["Key Vault Secrets User","Key Vault Administrator","Contributor","Key Vault Secrets Officer"],0,"Secrets User can read secret contents only. Secrets Officer can also create and delete, Administrator covers everything, and Contributor is management plane only.","Skills 4: Key Vault",[null,"Key Vault Administrator grants full data-plane control over keys, secrets and certificates, far beyond reading secrets.","Contributor is a management-plane role; in RBAC mode it does not grant reading secret values.","Secrets Officer can create, update and delete secrets, which is more than reading them."],1],["az71",0,4,"A developer accidentally deletes a Key Vault secret that production needs. Which feature lets them get it back?",["Access policies","Private endpoint","Key rotation policy","Soft delete (recover the deleted secret)"],3,"Soft delete keeps deleted secrets for a retention period so they can be recovered. Purge protection also stops them being purged early.","Skills 4: Key Vault",["Access policies control who can access the vault; they cannot restore a deleted secret.","A private endpoint limits network access and has nothing to do with recovering deleted data.","A rotation policy creates new key versions on a schedule; it applies to keys and cannot undelete a secret.",null],1],["az72",0,4,"An App Service app reads a database password from an app setting. The team wants the value to come from Key Vault without code changes. What should the setting contain?",["@Microsoft.KeyVault(SecretUri=https://myvault.vault.azure.net/secrets/DbPassword/)","keyvault://myvault/DbPassword","$(KeyVault.DbPassword)","The secret's version ID only"],0,"The @Microsoft.KeyVault(...) reference syntax makes App Service resolve the secret with the app's managed identity. The other forms are not recognized.","Skills 4: Key Vault references",[null,"keyvault:// is not a recognized reference syntax in App Service app settings.","$(KeyVault.DbPassword) looks like pipeline variable syntax; App Service does not resolve it.","A bare version ID does not tell App Service which vault and secret to read, and it is not the reference syntax."],2],["az73",0,4,"A team wants to be alerted automatically before a Key Vault secret expires so a function can rotate it. What should they use?",["An Application Insights availability test that calls the vault each hour","The Key Vault SecretNearExpiry event through Event Grid","A Cosmos DB change feed","A timer trigger that calls the portal"],1,"Key Vault publishes SecretNearExpiry events to Event Grid, which can trigger a function to rotate the secret. Availability tests check web endpoints.","Skills 4: Key Vault rotation",["Availability tests check whether web endpoints respond; they do not know when secrets will expire.",null,"The Cosmos DB change feed tracks item changes in Cosmos DB, not Key Vault secret expiry.","Calling the portal from a timer is not an API pattern, and polling is not the event-driven approach Key Vault offers."],2],["az74",0,4,"Several apps need the same settings with different values per environment (dev, test, prod) and feature flags that change without redeploying. Which service fits?",["Key Vault with one vault per setting","App Service deployment slots","Azure App Configuration with labels and feature flags","Environment variables baked into each container image at build time"],2,"App Configuration stores key-values with labels per environment and supports feature flags and dynamic refresh. Baked-in variables require a rebuild to change.","Skills 4: App Configuration",["Key Vault is for secrets; one vault per setting is costly and it has no labels or feature flags.","Deployment slots stage app versions and have sticky settings, but they do not share settings or feature flags across apps.",null,"Values baked in at build time need a rebuild and redeploy to change, which is what the team wants to avoid."],2],["az75",0,4,"An app loads App Configuration settings that include a Key Vault reference, but it fails to resolve the secret. The app can read App Configuration. What is missing?",["A label on the key","Soft delete on the vault","A sentinel key","Permission for the app's identity to read the secret in Key Vault"],3,"App Configuration stores only a pointer; the client fetches the secret from Key Vault with its own identity, so that identity needs a secret read role. Labels and sentinel keys do not affect access.","Skills 4: App Configuration",["Labels select environment-specific values; a missing label would not cause a Key Vault access failure.","Soft delete protects against deletion; it does not grant any read access.","A sentinel key triggers refresh; it has nothing to do with resolving a Key Vault reference.",null],3],["az76",0,4,"An app should reload many App Configuration settings only after a release is finished, not one key at a time. What pattern should be used?",["Poll every key every second","Watch a sentinel key and refresh all settings when it changes","Restart the app on every change","Store all settings as one JSON secret in Key Vault and read it at startup"],1,"Updating a single sentinel key after all changes signals the app to refresh everything at once, so it never sees a half-updated set.","Skills 4: App Configuration",["Polling every key every second wastes requests, can hit throttling and can load a half-updated set.",null,"Restarting on every change causes downtime and still loads settings one change at a time.","A single JSON secret in Key Vault read only at startup gives no dynamic refresh and puts non-secret settings in a vault."],3],["az77",0,4,"A Python web API must send traces, metrics and logs to Application Insights using OpenTelemetry. What is the simplest supported setup?",["Install the Log Analytics agent on the container","Write to stdout and query ContainerLog","Use the classic instrumentation key with HTTP data collector","Call configure_azure_monitor() from azure-monitor-opentelemetry with the connection string"],3,"The Azure Monitor OpenTelemetry Distro sets up exporters and common instrumentations with one call and a connection string. Instrumentation-key-only ingestion is retired.","Skills 4: OpenTelemetry",["The Log Analytics agent collects host and VM data, not in-app OpenTelemetry traces, and is retired.","Writing to stdout gives unstructured logs without traces, metrics or dependency correlation.","Instrumentation-key-only ingestion is retired in favor of connection strings, and the HTTP data collector API is for custom logs, not OpenTelemetry.",null],2],["az78",0,4,"A request passes through an API container, a Function and a database. Engineers want to see it as one end-to-end transaction in Application Insights. What makes this possible?",["Trace context propagated between services by OpenTelemetry","A separate Application Insights resource per service with no links","Sampling set to 0%","Writing the request ID into each log message by hand"],0,"OpenTelemetry passes W3C trace context in headers so spans share one trace ID and Application Insights can stitch them together. Sampling everything out would hide the data.","Skills 4: Distributed tracing",[null,"Separate resources with no link and no shared trace context cannot show the calls as one transaction.","Sampling at 0% would keep no telemetry at all, so there would be nothing to correlate.","Manually writing request IDs into logs is fragile and does not create spans or the transaction view."],3],["az79",0,4,"Which KQL query counts failed requests in 5-minute buckets over the last day?",["requests | where success == false | top 5 by timestamp","requests | where timestamp > ago(1d) and success == false | summarize count() by bin(timestamp, 5m)","exceptions | project timestamp | take 5","requests | summarize count() by success | where timestamp > ago(5m)"],1,"where filters the time and failures, and summarize count() by bin(timestamp, 5m) groups them into 5-minute buckets. top and take return rows, not counts per bucket.","Skills 4: KQL",["top 5 by timestamp returns the five latest failed rows, not counts per 5-minute bucket, and has no time range.",null,"This returns five exception timestamps with no counting, bucketing or failed-request filter.","It groups by success rather than time buckets, and after summarize the timestamp column no longer exists to filter."],2],["az80",0,4,"Which Application Insights table records outgoing calls from the app to SQL, HTTP APIs and storage?",["requests","traces","dependencies","pageViews"],2,"dependencies holds outbound calls with their target, duration and result. requests holds incoming calls to the app, and traces holds log messages.","Skills 4: KQL",["requests records incoming calls to the app, not outbound calls it makes.","traces holds log messages written by the app, not timed records of outbound calls.",null,"pageViews records browser page loads from client-side telemetry."],1],["az81",0,4,"A team wants an email when the error count in logs goes above 50 in 10 minutes, based on a KQL query. What should they create?",["A log search alert rule with an action group","A metric alert on CPU percentage","An availability test","A diagnostic setting that streams the logs to Event Hubs"],0,"A log search alert runs a KQL query on a schedule and fires an action group when the threshold is met. A CPU metric alert does not count log errors.","Skills 4: Alerts",[null,"A CPU metric alert fires on resource usage, not on the number of errors counted by a log query.","Availability tests probe a URL from outside; they do not count errors in the logs.","A diagnostic setting routes logs to a destination; it does not evaluate thresholds or send email."],2],["az82",0,4,"A Container App keeps restarting right after deployment. Where should the developer look first to see the app's own error output?",["The ACR task log","The Key Vault audit log","The Event Grid metrics blade","Container Apps console logs (log stream or ContainerAppConsoleLogs)"],3,"Console logs show what the container printed, including crash stack traces. System logs add platform events such as failed image pulls.","Skills 4: Troubleshooting",["The ACR task log shows image build output, not what the running container prints at startup.","The Key Vault audit log records vault access; it would show the app's own crash output only indirectly at best.","Event Grid metrics show event delivery counts and failures, unrelated to container restarts.",null],2],["az83",0,4,"An app sends too much telemetry, and Application Insights costs are rising. Which change reduces volume while keeping traces representative?",["Turn off the connection string","Configure sampling","Log every request body","Create one Application Insights resource per instance"],1,"Sampling keeps a representative share of telemetry and correlated items together, cutting ingestion cost. Disabling telemetry loses monitoring entirely.","Skills 4: Monitoring",["Removing the connection string stops all telemetry, losing monitoring entirely.",null,"Logging request bodies adds far more data and may capture sensitive information.","More Application Insights resources split the data but do not reduce total ingestion."],1],["az84",0,4,"A function app's managed identity must send messages to a Service Bus queue. Which role is the least-privilege choice?",["Owner on the namespace","Azure Service Bus Data Owner","Azure Service Bus Data Sender","Contributor on the resource group"],2,"Data Sender allows sending only. Data Owner allows send, receive and manage, and Owner or Contributor are management roles.","Skills 4: RBAC",["Owner is a management-plane role with full control; it is far beyond sending messages.","Data Owner allows send, receive and manage on the data plane, more than the task needs.",null,"Contributor manages resources in the whole resource group and does not follow least privilege for sending."],1],["az85",0,4,"What is the key difference between Key Vault secrets and keys?",["Secrets can only be 32 bytes long","Keys are exported as plain text to the app","Secrets are always hardware-protected","Secret values are returned to the app, while key operations run inside the vault"],3,"Apps read secret values, but with keys they send data to the vault to encrypt, decrypt, sign or wrap, and the private key material stays in the vault.","Skills 4: Key Vault",["Secrets can be up to 25 KB and are not limited to 32 bytes.","Private key material never leaves the vault in plain text; apps call the vault to use the key.","Secrets are stored encrypted but are not always hardware-protected; HSM protection is a feature of keys (Premium or Managed HSM).",null],1],["az86",0,4,"Which statement about system-assigned managed identities is correct?",["They are deleted when the resource they belong to is deleted","They can be attached to many resources","They require a client secret in app settings","They must be rotated every 90 days by the developer using a script"],0,"A system-assigned identity shares the life cycle of its resource. User-assigned identities can be shared, and Azure handles credentials for both types.","Skills 4: Managed identity",[null,"Attaching one identity to many resources describes user-assigned identities; a system-assigned identity belongs to one resource.","Managed identities need no client secret; Azure manages their credentials.","Azure rotates managed identity credentials automatically; developers never rotate them with scripts."],1],["az87",0,4,"A Python app deployed to App Service returns HTTP 500 errors, and the team needs to see live output while they reproduce the issue. What should they use?",["Deployment slot swap","Log stream after turning on application logging","ACR webhooks","The Cosmos DB metrics blade"],1,"The log stream shows application and web server logs in near real time once logging is enabled. Slot swaps change versions and do not show errors.","Skills 4: Troubleshooting",["A slot swap changes which version is live; it does not show error output while reproducing the issue.",null,"ACR webhooks notify on image push or delete events; they do not show application logs.","Cosmos DB metrics show database throughput and errors, not the web app's own 500 errors and output."],1],["az88",0,4,"A developer wants to check that the Cosmos DB account allows data-plane access only with Microsoft Entra identities, not account keys. What should be configured?",["Session consistency on the account","A private endpoint only","Disable local (key-based) authentication and assign Cosmos DB data-plane roles","An App Configuration feature flag"],2,"Turning off local authentication forces Entra ID with data-plane role assignments. A private endpoint limits the network path but still allows keys.","Skills 4: RBAC",["Consistency level affects read guarantees and has nothing to do with authentication methods.","A private endpoint restricts the network path but still allows account keys to be used.",null,"An App Configuration feature flag toggles app behavior; it cannot change how Cosmos DB authenticates requests."],3],["x1",0,1,"Every small code change to a Python API makes docker build reinstall all pip packages, which takes minutes. The Dockerfile runs COPY . . and then pip install -r requirements.txt. What change helps most?",["Add the --no-cache flag to docker build so every layer is rebuilt cleanly each time","Copy requirements.txt and run pip install before copying the rest of the code","Move the pip install into a CMD instruction so it runs when the container starts","Switch the base image tag from a slim variant to the full python image variant"],1,"Docker reuses a cached layer until an earlier instruction's inputs change. Copying only requirements.txt first means the pip install layer is reused unless dependencies change. --no-cache is tempting for clean builds but defeats caching entirely.","Domain 1: Dockerfile layer caching",["Disabling the cache makes every build slower; the goal is to let Docker reuse the dependency layer.",null,"Installing packages at startup slows every container start and makes images unreproducible.","A larger base image adds size and does nothing to preserve the pip layer between code changes."],2],["x2",0,1,"A developer's image accidentally contains the local .env file, the .git folder and a virtual environment, because the Dockerfile uses COPY . . for the app code. What is the simplest fix?",["Delete those files with RUN rm in a later layer","Mark the files read-only on the build machine","Push the image to a private registry instead","List those paths in a .dockerignore file"],3,"A .dockerignore file excludes paths from the build context, so they never reach COPY instructions or any layer. RUN rm is tempting, but the files persist in the lower layer where they were added.","Domain 1: .dockerignore",["Files deleted in a later layer still exist in the earlier layer, so secrets remain in the image.","File permissions on the host do not stop them from being sent in the build context and copied.","A private registry limits who can pull, but the secrets are still baked into the image.",null],1],["x3",0,1,"A security review finds that a Python container runs its web server as root. The image is based on python:3.12-slim. How should the developer change the Dockerfile?",["Create an unprivileged user and add a USER instruction before CMD","Add EXPOSE 8000 so the app no longer needs root privileges","Run chmod 777 on the app folder so any user can execute it","Use ENTRYPOINT instead of CMD to start the web server process"],0,"Creating a dedicated user and switching to it with USER makes the process run without root privileges, limiting impact if it is compromised. EXPOSE is tempting because ports are linked to privilege in people's minds, but it is documentation only.","Domain 1: Non-root containers",[null,"EXPOSE only documents a port; it does not change which user the process runs as.","World-writable files weaken security further and do not change the running user.","ENTRYPOINT versus CMD changes how arguments are passed, not the process user."],2],["x4",0,1,"A team deploys container images by the tag 'prod' and later cannot tell which build is running or roll back reliably. What tagging practice should they adopt for deployments?",["One stable tag like 'prod' that is overwritten by each release","The 'latest' tag for every build pushed to the container registry","A unique tag per build, such as the build ID or commit SHA","A date-only tag reused for all builds that happen on the same day"],2,"Unique tags never get overwritten, so each deployment points at a known build and rollbacks just redeploy an earlier tag (or digest). Stable tags suit base images that should receive patches, not deployments that must be traceable.","Domain 1: Stable vs unique tags",["Overwriting one tag is the current problem: the tag no longer identifies a specific build.","latest is just another mutable tag and hides which build is actually deployed.",null,"Several builds a day would share one tag, so the tag still does not identify a build."],2],["x5",0,1,"A team wants their image rebuilt in Azure and pushed to ACR automatically whenever code is committed to the main branch of their Git repository, without running their own build agents. What should they create?",["An ACR webhook that fires when an image is pushed to the registry","A Container Apps job that runs docker build on a fixed schedule","An ACR task with a source code commit trigger on the repository","A geo-replicated Premium registry with content trust turned on"],2,"ACR tasks can be triggered by source commits (and pull requests) in a Git repository, building and pushing images with no self-hosted agents. Webhooks are tempting but react to registry events after an image already exists.","Domain 1: ACR Tasks",["ACR webhooks fire after registry events like pushes; they cannot start a build from a Git commit.","A scheduled job rebuilds on a timer, not per commit, and docker is not available inside it by default.",null,"Geo-replication and content trust affect distribution and signing, not automatic builds."],2],["x6",0,1,"An App Service web app runs a custom container from ACR. The team wants the app to pull and restart automatically whenever a new image is pushed to the tag it uses. What should they enable?",["Continuous deployment for the container in Deployment Center","Always On for the web app in its general configuration","Automatic OS patching on the App Service plan's workers","Autoscale rules for the App Service plan on CPU usage"],0,"Enabling continuous deployment for a container configures a webhook so App Service pulls the updated image and restarts when the tag is pushed. Always On is tempting because it sounds like it keeps things current, but it only prevents idle unloading.","Domain 1: App Service continuous deployment",[null,"Always On keeps the app loaded but never pulls a newer image.","Platform patching updates the host, not the container image the app runs.","Autoscale adds instances; it does not detect or pull new image versions."],2],["x7",0,1,"An App Service app has a staging slot pointed at a test database and a production slot pointed at the production database, both via an app setting. After a swap, production suddenly uses the test database. What should the developer change?",["Put the database string directly in the container image of each slot","Swap back and scale the production slot out to more instances","Use a separate App Service plan for the staging deployment slot","Mark the connection setting as a deployment slot setting in both slots"],3,"Settings marked as deployment slot settings stay with the slot during a swap, so each environment keeps its own database. Without the flag, the value moves with the swapped content. A separate plan is tempting but unrelated to settings behavior.","Domain 1: Deployment slots",["Baking settings into images ties images to environments and moves the setting with every swap.","Scaling out does not stop settings from moving during the next swap.","Slots can share or not share a plan; the plan has no effect on which settings swap.",null],3],["x8",0,1,"A developer updates a secret value on a Container App, but the running revision keeps using the old value. What explains this, and what should they do?",["Secrets are cached by the registry; purge the image and push again","Secret changes do not create a revision; restart or deploy a new one","Secrets refresh only in single revision mode; switch the app to it first","Secret updates are applied only after the environment is recreated"],1,"Secrets are an application-scope setting: changing them does not create a revision, and existing revisions pick up the new value only after a restart or a new revision. Assuming the registry caches secrets is tempting but wrong.","Domain 1: Container Apps secrets and revisions",["Secrets are not stored in the image or registry, so purging images changes nothing.",null,"Revision mode controls how many revisions run; it does not refresh secrets on its own.","Recreating the environment is drastic and unnecessary; restarting the revision is enough."],3],["x9",0,1,"A Container App in single revision mode is updated with a new image. What happens to traffic?",["A new revision is created and receives all traffic once ready","The new revision is created with 0% traffic until assigned","Both revisions split traffic evenly until one is deleted","The image is replaced in place without any new revision"],0,"In single revision mode, a revision-scope change creates a new revision that takes 100% of traffic when it is ready, and the old one is deactivated. The 0% option is tempting because it describes multiple revision mode.","Domain 1: Container Apps revision modes",[null,"Zero-traffic revisions are a multiple revision mode behavior, not single revision mode.","Even splitting needs multiple revision mode with explicit weights.","Changing the image is a revision-scope change, so it always creates a new revision."],2],["x10",0,1,"An API on Container Apps scales to zero at night, and the first morning requests are slow while a replica starts. Latency matters more than the small idle cost. What should be changed?",["Lower the HTTP rule's concurrency value","Use a larger container image for the app","Set the minimum replica count to 1","Switch the app to internal ingress mode"],2,"With minimum replicas at 1, one replica always stays running, so the first request does not wait for a cold start. Tuning the HTTP concurrency is tempting, but scaling rules only act once traffic arrives.","Domain 1: Container Apps scaling",["A lower concurrency target scales out sooner under load but still starts from zero after idle.","A bigger image typically starts slower, making the cold start worse.",null,"Ingress visibility has no effect on whether a replica is already running."],2],["x11",0,1,"Each message in a Service Bus queue needs up to 30 minutes of container processing, then the container should exit. When the queue is empty, nothing should run. Which Container Apps option fits best?",["A container app with an HTTP scale rule and min 0","An event-driven job with a Service Bus scale rule","A scheduled job that runs every 30 minutes all day","A container app with minimum replicas fixed at 1"],1,"Event-driven Container Apps jobs start executions based on a KEDA scaler such as Service Bus queue length, run to completion and stop. A container app with a scale rule is tempting, but apps are for long-running services rather than run-to-completion work.","Domain 1: Container Apps jobs",["HTTP scale rules react to HTTP traffic, not queue backlog, and apps are meant to keep running rather than exit.",null,"A schedule runs whether or not messages exist and may lag behind bursts.","A permanently running replica costs money when the queue is empty, which the requirement rules out."],3],["x12",0,1,"An AKS pod keeps restarting, and kubectl describe shows the last state as OOMKilled. What is the most likely cause?",["The pod's CPU request was higher than any node had","The image could not be pulled from the private container registry","The Service selector does not match the pod labels","The container used more memory than its memory limit"],3,"OOMKilled means the container exceeded its memory limit and the kernel terminated it. Raise the limit or reduce usage. An oversized CPU request is tempting since it is also a resource setting, but it keeps the pod Pending instead.","Domain 1: AKS requests and limits",["An unschedulable request leaves the pod Pending; it would never start and be killed.","A pull failure shows ErrImagePull or ImagePullBackOff, not OOMKilled.","A selector mismatch stops traffic reaching the pod but does not kill the container.",null],2],["x13",0,1,"A Kubernetes Deployment needs a database password and a non-sensitive feature toggle as environment variables. Which objects should hold them?",["A ConfigMap for both values, since both are configuration","Both values written into the container image at build time","A Service object for the password and labels for the toggle","A Secret for the password and a ConfigMap for the toggle"],3,"Kubernetes Secrets are designed for sensitive values and ConfigMaps for non-confidential configuration; both can be injected as environment variables. Using a ConfigMap for everything is tempting but treats the password as plain configuration.","Domain 1: AKS ConfigMaps and Secrets",["ConfigMaps are meant for non-confidential data, so the password should not go there.","Baking values into images exposes the password and forces a rebuild for any change.","Services expose pods on the network, and labels identify objects; neither stores config.",null],1],["x14",0,1,"An AKS Deployment's pods must be reachable only by other workloads inside the cluster through a stable name. Which Service type fits?",["LoadBalancer","ClusterIP","NodePort","ExternalName"],1,"ClusterIP is the default Service type: it gives a stable internal IP and DNS name reachable only inside the cluster. LoadBalancer is tempting because it is common, but it exposes the service externally.","Domain 1: AKS Services",["LoadBalancer provisions an external IP, exposing the pods outside the cluster.",null,"NodePort opens a port on every node, which exposes the service beyond cluster workloads.","ExternalName maps to an outside DNS name; it does not front the Deployment's pods."],1],["x15",0,2,"Two users update the same Cosmos DB item almost at once, and one change is silently lost. How should the Python code prevent this?",["Switch the account's consistency level from Session to Strong","Use upsert_item instead of replace_item for every update","Replace the item using its _etag with an if-match condition","Add a composite index on the fields being updated"],2,"Optimistic concurrency sends the _etag read earlier with an if-match condition; if another writer changed the item, the write fails with a precondition error and the app can retry. Strong consistency is tempting but does not guard writes.","Domain 2: Cosmos DB optimistic concurrency",["Stronger consistency changes what reads see, not whether a later write overwrites an earlier one.","Upsert still overwrites the whole item, so the lost-update problem remains.",null,"Indexes affect query cost, not write conflicts."],2],["x16",0,2,"A query sorts Cosmos DB items with ORDER BY c.category ASC, c.price DESC and fails. What should the developer add to the indexing policy?",["A composite index on category ascending and price descending","A spatial index on the category and price properties","A vector index on the price property of each item","An excluded path for both properties used in the sort"],0,"ORDER BY on multiple properties requires a composite index whose paths and sort orders match the query. A vector index is tempting in an AI-focused container, but it only serves VectorDistance searches.","Domain 2: Cosmos DB indexing policy",[null,"Spatial indexes support geospatial queries, not multi-property sorting.","Vector indexes support similarity search on embeddings, not ORDER BY on scalar values.","Excluding the paths removes index support, making the query less able to run."],2],["x17",0,2,"Chat session items in Cosmos DB should be deleted automatically 7 days after their last update, with no cleanup code. What should the developer configure?",["A change feed processor that deletes items older than 7 days","Time to live on the container, set to 7 days in seconds","A timer-triggered function that queries and deletes old items","An indexing policy that excludes items older than 7 days"],1,"Setting a default TTL on the container (in seconds) makes Cosmos DB delete items automatically after that time since their last write, using spare RUs. A timer function is the tempting DIY answer but adds code and RU cost.","Domain 2: Cosmos DB TTL",["The change feed reports changes; using it to delete old items is custom code and does not fire for idle items.",null,"A scheduled delete job works but is exactly the cleanup code the requirement wants to avoid.","Indexing policies cannot exclude items by age; they control which paths are indexed."],2],["x18",0,2,"An index must stay in sync with a Cosmos DB container, including removing entries when items are deleted outright (no soft-delete flag). Which change feed option supports this?",["Latest version mode with a lease","Change feed with Strong consistency","A Cosmos DB trigger with a larger batch","All versions and deletes mode"],3,"All versions and deletes mode records every change, including deletes, within the account's continuous backup retention window. Latest version mode is the default and is tempting, but it omits deletes, which is why soft-delete flags are often used with it.","Domain 2: Change feed modes",["Latest version mode does not emit deletes, whatever lease setup is used.","Consistency level does not change which operations appear in the change feed.","The Functions trigger uses latest version mode, so batch size does not surface deletes.",null],3],["x19",0,2,"A dashboard for a single tenant queries a large Cosmos DB container with enable_cross_partition_query=True and no partition key, and RU charges are high. tenantId is the container partition key path. What should the developer change?",["Pass the tenant's partition key value so the query hits one partition","Raise max_item_count so each page of results returns more items at once","Raise the container's provisioned RU/s to cover the extra fan-out cost","Lower the consistency level to Eventual for all of the dashboard reads"],0,"Supplying the partition key (in the filter or partition_key argument) scopes the query to one logical partition, avoiding a costly fan-out. Raising the page size is tempting because it cuts round trips, but the fan-out cost remains.","Domain 2: Cosmos DB request units",[null,"Bigger pages mean fewer round trips, but every partition is still queried and charged.","More RU/s pays for the waste instead of removing it.","Weaker consistency can trim some read cost but leaves the cross-partition fan-out in place."],2],["x20",0,2,"A global app reads from Cosmos DB in several regions. It needs reads that are never more than a known number of versions or time behind writes, with lower latency than Strong. Which consistency level fits?",["Session","Consistent prefix","Bounded staleness","Eventual"],2,"Bounded staleness guarantees reads lag writes by no more than a configured number of versions or time interval, while avoiding the latency cost of Strong. Session is tempting because it is the default, but its guarantee is per session.","Domain 2: Cosmos DB consistency levels",["Session guarantees read-your-own-writes for one client session, not a bound on lag for all readers.","Consistent prefix preserves write order but sets no limit on how far behind reads can be.",null,"Eventual gives no ordering or staleness guarantee at all."],3],["x21",0,2,"A RAG app stores document chunks with embeddings from one model. After the team switches query embeddings to a different embedding model, similarity results become meaningless. What is the cause?",["The vector index must be rebuilt after every new user question","Cosine distance cannot be used with vectors that were normalized","Query and stored vectors must come from the same embedding model","The chunks are too short to be compared with longer user questions"],2,"Embeddings from different models occupy different vector spaces (and may differ in dimensions), so distances between them are not comparable. Re-embed the stored chunks with the new model or keep the old one for queries. Rebuilding the index is tempting but does not fix mismatched spaces.","Domain 2: RAG embeddings",["Indexes do not need rebuilding per query; the problem is that the vectors live in different spaces.","Normalized vectors work well with cosine distance; normalization is not the issue.",null,"Chunk length affects retrieval quality, but it does not make results meaningless across models."],2],["x22",0,2,"When preparing long documents for RAG, why do teams often split text into chunks with a small overlap between neighboring chunks?",["So sentences near a boundary keep their context in at least one chunk","So the vector index uses less storage than it would if chunks had no overlap","So each chunk can use a different embedding model and be compared","So documents no longer need any metadata such as source or owner"],0,"Overlap keeps ideas that straddle a chunk boundary intact in at least one chunk, which improves retrieval. It costs a little extra storage, so the storage-saving option is a tempting but backwards reason.","Domain 2: RAG chunking",[null,"Overlap duplicates some text, so it slightly increases storage rather than reducing it.","All chunks must share one embedding model for similarity to be meaningful.","Metadata is still needed for filtering, citations and access control."],1],["x23",0,2,"A pgvector table has an HNSW index created with vector_l2_ops. Queries use ORDER BY embedding <=> $1 LIMIT 5 and are slow, with the plan showing a sequential scan. Why?",["HNSW indexes only work when the query also filters on a B-tree column","LIMIT 5 is too small; HNSW is used only for LIMIT values over 100","The query must use <#> because HNSW supports only inner product","The <=> cosine operator does not match the L2 operator class of the index"],3,"A pgvector index serves only the distance operator of its operator class: vector_l2_ops for <->, vector_cosine_ops for <=>, vector_ip_ops for <#>. Create a cosine index or query with <->. Blaming the LIMIT is tempting but not how the planner decides.","Domain 2: pgvector operators and indexes",["HNSW indexes do not require an extra B-tree filter to be used.","There is no such LIMIT threshold; small LIMIT values are the typical index use case.","HNSW supports L2, cosine and inner product, each through its own operator class.",null],3],["x24",0,2,"In pgvector, which operator returns the negative inner product between two vectors?",["<->","<#>","<=>","<+>"],1,"pgvector's <#> returns the negative inner product, so ORDER BY ascending still puts the most similar vectors first. <=> is the tempting choice because cosine and inner product are closely related, but it returns cosine distance.","Domain 2: pgvector distance operators",["<-> returns Euclidean (L2) distance.",null,"<=> returns cosine distance.","<+> is the L1 (taxicab) distance operator, not inner product."],1],["x25",0,2,"A team must add an approximate nearest neighbor index to a pgvector table that is still empty and will fill gradually over months. Which index type is the better fit, and why?",["HNSW, because it needs no training step on existing rows","IVFFlat, because its lists are learned from an empty table","A B-tree, because it handles vector ranges most efficiently","IVFFlat with probes set to 0 so no lists need to be searched"],0,"HNSW builds its graph incrementally as rows arrive and has no training phase, so it can be created on an empty table. IVFFlat clusters existing data into lists, so it should be built after loading data. IVFFlat is tempting because it builds faster.","Domain 2: pgvector HNSW vs IVFFlat",[null,"IVFFlat computes its lists from the rows present at build time, so building on an empty table gives poor recall.","B-tree indexes do not support vector similarity search.","Zero probes would search no lists at all; probes must be at least 1."],3],["x26",0,2,"An app uses cache-aside with Azure Managed Redis in front of PostgreSQL. When a product is updated, users see the old version until the key expires. What should the update code do?",["Write to Redis only and let PostgreSQL catch up some time later","Turn off TTL on product keys so they are never evicted by Redis","Write to the database, then delete or update that product's key","Flush the entire cache after every single change to any product"],2,"With cache-aside, invalidating (or updating) the specific key after a database write removes stale data while keeping the rest of the cache warm. Flushing the whole cache is tempting for correctness but punishes the database.","Domain 2: Redis cache-aside invalidation",["Writing only to Redis makes the cache the source of truth and risks losing data.","Removing TTLs makes staleness worse, since keys would never refresh on their own.",null,"Flushing everything works but causes a burst of cache misses and database load after every change."],2],["x27",0,2,"An Azure Managed Redis instance used purely as a cache becomes full, and writes start failing with out-of-memory errors. Every key could be recomputed. Which eviction policy should be set?",["noeviction","allkeys-lru","volatile-ttl","volatile-random"],1,"allkeys-lru lets Redis evict any key, least recently used first, which suits a cache where everything can be rebuilt. The volatile policies are tempting, but they only consider keys that have an expiry set.","Domain 2: Redis eviction policies",["noeviction is what causes write errors when memory is full.",null,"volatile-ttl only evicts keys with a TTL; keys without one can still fill memory.","volatile-random also ignores keys without a TTL, so writes can still fail."],2],["x28",0,2,"A semantic cache in Redis returns stored answers for new questions whose embeddings fall within a similarity threshold. Users report answers to questions they did not ask. What should be adjusted?",["Lengthen the cache TTL so entries are stored for longer","Switch the eviction policy to noeviction to keep entries","Increase the cache size so more answers can be stored","Tighten the similarity threshold so only close matches count as hits"],3,"A threshold that is too loose treats merely related questions as the same, returning their cached answers. A stricter threshold trades some hit rate for correctness. Changing TTL is tempting because it is the usual cache knob, but it affects freshness, not matching.","Domain 2: Semantic caching",["A longer TTL keeps entries around longer; it does nothing about loose matches.","noeviction affects what happens when memory is full, not how matches are chosen.","More capacity stores more entries but the same loose threshold still returns wrong answers.",null],3],["x29",0,2,"A container of product documents uses a vector policy with 1,536-dimension embeddings, and the developer wants approximate search that keeps RU cost and latency low as the collection grows. Which vector index type suits this best?",["flat on the embedding path","range on /embedding/*","spatial on the embedding path","quantizedFlat or diskANN"],3,"quantizedFlat compresses vectors for cheaper brute-force search, and diskANN is built for large-scale approximate search with low latency. flat is tempting because it gives exact results, but it becomes costly as the collection grows.","Domain 2: Cosmos DB vector indexes",["A flat index does exact brute-force comparison, so it scales poorly and is meant for small vector counts.","Range indexes are for scalar filtering and sorting, not vector similarity.","Spatial indexes support geographic data types, not embeddings.",null],2],["x30",0,2,"A developer calls container.upsert_item(body) with the azure-cosmos Python SDK. What does this do?",["Creates the item and fails with a conflict if the id already exists anywhere","Creates the item, or replaces it if the id already exists in that partition","Patches only the fields in the body and keeps other properties unchanged","Inserts the item into every partition so point reads can find it faster"],1,"upsert_item creates the item if it does not exist, otherwise it replaces the existing item with the same id and partition key. create_item is the tempting mix-up because it also writes new items, but it fails on conflicts.","Domain 2: Cosmos DB Python SDK",["That describes create_item, which raises a conflict error on an existing id.",null,"Upsert replaces the whole item; partial updates use patch operations.","Each item lives in one logical partition, determined by its partition key value."],1],["x31",0,2,"An app already stores its user profiles and chat history in Cosmos DB for NoSQL and now needs similarity search over embeddings of those same documents, with filters on user properties. What is the most suitable approach?",["Copy all documents into PostgreSQL with pgvector nightly","Keep all embeddings only in Azure Managed Redis memory","Add a vector policy and index to the Cosmos DB container","Store embeddings in Blob Storage and scan them in code"],2,"Cosmos DB for NoSQL supports vector policies, vector indexes and VectorDistance(), so embeddings can live beside the documents and be filtered with normal WHERE clauses. pgvector is tempting and strong for relational data, but moving this NoSQL data adds sync and staleness.","Domain 2: Choosing a vector store",["A nightly copy adds a second database to run and leaves search results up to a day stale.","Redis as the only store for this data risks losing it and duplicates the data model.",null,"Scanning blobs in application code is slow and does not scale."],3],["x32",0,3,"Expired Service Bus messages disappear instead of showing up in the queue's dead-letter subqueue. The team wants to inspect them. What should they enable?",["Dead-lettering on message expiration for the queue","Duplicate detection with a longer detection history window","Sessions on the queue with a session per sender","Auto-forwarding from the queue to a second queue"],0,"By default, expired messages are removed. Enabling dead-lettering on message expiration moves them to the $DeadLetterQueue subqueue for inspection. Auto-forwarding is tempting because it moves messages, but it does not apply to expiry.","Domain 3: Service Bus dead-lettering",[null,"Duplicate detection drops repeated messages; it does not preserve expired ones.","Sessions provide ordering per session and have nothing to do with expiry handling.","Auto-forwarding moves new messages elsewhere; expired messages would still be dropped."],2],["x33",0,3,"A Python service must read messages from a Service Bus queue's dead-letter subqueue to diagnose failures. How should it open the receiver?",["Receive from the main queue with receive-and-delete mode enabled","Use get_queue_receiver with sub_queue set to the dead-letter subqueue","Create a new queue named deadletter and receive from that queue","Peek the main queue with a filter for messages with delivery count 10"],1,"In the Python SDK, get_queue_receiver(queue_name, sub_queue=ServiceBusSubQueue.DEAD_LETTER) reads the <queue>/$DeadLetterQueue subqueue. Creating a queue named deadletter is tempting but it is unrelated to the real subqueue.","Domain 3: Reading the dead-letter queue",["Receiving from the main queue only returns active messages, never dead-lettered ones.",null,"Dead-lettered messages are in a subqueue of the original queue, not a separate queue you create.","Peeking the main queue cannot see messages that have already moved to the dead-letter subqueue."],2],["x34",0,3,"An order handler receives a payment-confirmed message before the matching order-created message. It wants to set the payment message aside and fetch that exact message later by its sequence number. What should it do?",["Abandon the message so it is redelivered to another receiver","Dead-letter the message and resubmit it after the order arrives","Complete the message and store a copy in a local memory list","Defer the message and later receive it by sequence number"],3,"Deferral keeps the message in the queue but hidden from normal receives; the app records the sequence number and later calls receive_deferred_messages. Abandon is tempting but just returns the message to the queue.","Domain 3: Service Bus deferral",["Abandoning makes it available again immediately, often looping back and raising the delivery count.","Dead-lettering signals failure and needs extra handling to resubmit; deferral is built for this case.","Completing removes it from Service Bus, so a crash would lose the in-memory copy.",null],3],["x35",0,3,"A reminder message must become visible to receivers exactly at 09:00 tomorrow, not before. What Service Bus feature should the sender use?",["Scheduled messages","Message time-to-live","Session state","Duplicate detection"],0,"Scheduling a message (schedule_messages or a scheduled enqueue time) keeps it invisible until the given time. TTL is tempting because it is also time-based, but it defines when a message expires.","Domain 3: Service Bus scheduled messages",[null,"TTL controls when a message expires, not when it first becomes visible.","Session state stores data for a session and does not delay delivery.","Duplicate detection drops repeat sends and has no timing effect on delivery."],1],["x36",0,3,"A topic subscription should receive only messages whose correlation ID equals 'eu-orders', and the team wants the most efficient filter type. What should they use?",["A SQL filter using a LIKE pattern on the label","A topic per region created by the message sender","A correlation filter on the correlation ID","A session ID filter on the subscription receiver"],2,"Correlation filters match message properties such as CorrelationId by equality and are cheaper to evaluate than SQL filters. A SQL filter is tempting because it can express the same rule, but it is less efficient.","Domain 3: Service Bus subscription filters",["SQL filters work but are more expensive to evaluate than simple equality correlation filters.","Adding topics moves routing into the sender and multiplies entities to manage.",null,"There is no session ID filter; sessions group messages for ordered processing."],2],["x37",0,3,"An Event Grid subscription must deliver only events where the data field orderTotal is greater than 1000. What should be configured on the subscription?",["A subject begins-with filter using the value 1000","An event type filter set to orderTotal","An advanced filter using NumberGreaterThan on data.orderTotal","A dead-letter destination for smaller orders"],2,"Advanced filters can compare fields in the event, including data properties, with operators such as NumberGreaterThan. Subject filters are the tempting choice because they are the most common filter, but they only match subject text.","Domain 3: Event Grid advanced filters",["Subject filters compare the subject string prefix, not numeric data values.","Event type filters match the eventType field, not values inside data.",null,"Dead-lettering stores undeliverable events; it does not filter what is delivered."],2],["x38",0,3,"A team needs to pick services for three flows: (1) transactional order commands that must not be lost and need ordered processing per customer, (2) reacting to blob-created events across many subscribers, (3) ingesting high-volume telemetry for stream processing. Which mapping fits?",["(1) Service Bus, (2) Event Grid, (3) Event Hubs","(1) Event Grid, (2) Service Bus, (3) Event Hubs","(1) Event Hubs, (2) Event Grid, (3) Service Bus","(1) Service Bus, (2) Event Hubs, (3) Event Grid"],0,"Service Bus handles reliable enterprise messaging with sessions and dead-lettering; Event Grid routes discrete events such as BlobCreated to many subscribers; Event Hubs ingests high-throughput streams for replay. Swapping Event Grid and Service Bus is the most common mistake.","Domain 3: Choosing Service Bus, Event Grid or Event Hubs",[null,"Event Grid is for reactive event distribution, not transactional commands needing sessions.","Event Hubs lacks per-message settlement, sessions and dead-lettering for commands, and Service Bus is not built for high-volume telemetry.","Event Hubs is not the native route for Azure resource events, and Event Grid is not a telemetry ingestion stream."],3],["x39",0,3,"A function app needs a longer execution timeout and a limit on how many Service Bus messages each instance processes at once. Where are these settings configured?",["local.settings.json","function_app.py","requirements.txt","host.json"],3,"host.json holds runtime-wide settings such as functionTimeout and extension options like Service Bus concurrency. local.settings.json is tempting because it is also a settings file, but it only feeds app settings during local development.","Domain 3: Functions host.json",["local.settings.json holds app settings for local runs only and is not deployed.","function_app.py declares functions and bindings, not host-wide runtime options.","requirements.txt lists Python packages to install.",null],2],["x40",0,3,"A developer has a Python function app project ready locally and an existing function app in Azure named orders-func. Which Core Tools command deploys the code?",["func start --publish orders-func","func azure functionapp publish orders-func","func new --deploy orders-func","func init orders-func --worker-runtime python"],1,"func azure functionapp publish <app-name> packages the project and deploys it to the named function app. func start is tempting because it is the most used command, but it only runs the host locally.","Domain 3: Functions deployment",["func start runs the functions locally and has no publish option.",null,"func new creates a new function from a template; it does not deploy.","func init creates a new local project rather than deploying an existing one."],1],["x41",0,3,"A function app must use its managed identity instead of a connection string for its host storage account. Which app setting shape enables this?",["AzureWebJobsStorage__accountName set to the storage account name","AzureWebJobsStorage set to the account key taken from the portal","FUNCTIONS_WORKER_RUNTIME set to the value managedidentity","WEBSITE_RUN_FROM_PACKAGE set to the storage account's URL"],0,"Identity-based connections use settings with a double-underscore suffix, such as AzureWebJobsStorage__accountName, and the identity needs data roles on the account. Putting the key in AzureWebJobsStorage is tempting because it is the classic setup.","Domain 3: Identity-based connections",[null,"An account key is exactly the shared secret the requirement wants to remove.","FUNCTIONS_WORKER_RUNTIME selects the language worker and has no identity values.","Run from package points at a deployment package; it does not configure host storage auth."],2],["x42",0,3,"A Python v2 function must run when an Event Grid event is delivered and read the event's data. Which declaration fits?",["A function.json file with an eventGridTrigger binding next to function_app.py","An @app.route decorator with an HTTP trigger that Event Grid polls regularly","An @app.event_grid_trigger decorator with a func.EventGridEvent parameter","An @app.timer_trigger decorator that checks the Event Grid topic every minute"],2,"In the v2 model, @app.event_grid_trigger(arg_name=...) binds an EventGridEvent whose get_json() returns the data. function.json is tempting from older samples, but v2 apps declare triggers in code.","Domain 3: Functions Python v2 triggers",["The v2 model declares bindings with decorators; function.json belongs to the v1 model.","Event Grid pushes events; it does not poll HTTP endpoints, and the dedicated trigger handles validation for you.",null,"Timers cannot pull events from an Event Grid topic, which pushes to subscribers."],2],["x43",0,3,"A function app on the Consumption plan has slow first requests after idle periods, and it must also reach a database through a virtual network. The team accepts paying for warm capacity. Which plan fits best?",["Consumption plan with a longer timeout","Premium plan with always-ready instances","Dedicated plan with autoscale turned off","Consumption plan with a timer keep-alive"],1,"The Premium plan keeps always-ready instances warm to avoid cold starts and supports VNet integration. A keep-alive timer is tempting as a cheap trick, but it is unreliable and does not add network access.","Domain 3: Functions hosting plans",["A longer timeout lets executions run longer but does nothing about cold starts or VNet access.",null,"A Dedicated plan without autoscale cannot respond to load, which is a poor fit for event-driven work.","A keep-alive timer is a fragile workaround and still leaves no VNet integration on classic Consumption."],3],["x44",0,3,"A Python v2 function reads a queue message and must return an HTTP response and also write a document to Cosmos DB. How should the Cosmos DB write be declared?",["As a Cosmos DB input binding decorator with the document as its value","As a host.json setting that names the database and target container","As an app setting that starts with CosmosDBOutput__ for that function","As a Cosmos DB output binding decorator, setting the output parameter"],3,"Output bindings (for example @app.cosmos_db_output) declare the target, and the function sets the output parameter's value, so no SDK code is needed. An input binding is tempting because it names the same container, but it only reads.","Domain 3: Functions output bindings",["Input bindings read data into the function; they do not write documents.","host.json holds runtime settings, not per-function bindings.","App settings supply connection values; they cannot declare a binding by themselves.",null],2],["x45",0,3,"A subscriber processes Event Grid events, and the team is worried that during a long outage, events will be dropped after retries end. Which two settings on the event subscription control this?",["Subject filters and the choice of CloudEvents schema","Webhook validation and the maximum batch size used","Advanced filters and the topic's input schema type","Retry policy (max attempts, event TTL) and a dead-letter location"],3,"Event Grid retries until the maximum delivery attempts or event time-to-live is reached; with a dead-letter storage container configured, undelivered events are kept there instead of being dropped. Webhook validation is tempting since it causes failures, but only when subscribing.","Domain 3: Event Grid delivery and dead-lettering",["Filters and schema affect what is delivered and its format, not what happens after failed retries.","Validation proves endpoint ownership, and batching affects throughput, not retention of failed events.","Filters and input schema do not change how long Event Grid retries or where failures go.",null],3],["x46",0,3,"A Service Bus consumer can tolerate losing a message if it crashes mid-processing, and it wants the lowest overhead per message. Which receive mode fits?",["Peek-lock","Receive-and-delete","Deferred receive","Session receive"],1,"Receive-and-delete removes the message as soon as it is delivered, avoiding the settle round-trip, but a crash loses it, which this consumer accepts. Peek-lock is the tempting default and is right when messages must not be lost.","Domain 3: Service Bus receive modes",["Peek-lock needs a separate settle call per message, adding overhead the app does not need.",null,"Deferral is for setting specific messages aside, not a general low-overhead receive.","Sessions add ordered, locked processing per session, which is extra overhead here."],1],["x47",0,4,"A container app has two user-assigned managed identities. DefaultAzureCredential authenticates as the wrong one when accessing Key Vault. What should the developer do?",["Remove the Key Vault Secrets User role from the environment's identity","Switch both identities to system-assigned identities on the same app","Pass managed_identity_client_id or set AZURE_CLIENT_ID to the right identity","Store a client secret for the right identity in the app's configuration"],2,"When several user-assigned identities are attached, specify which one to use: set AZURE_CLIENT_ID or pass managed_identity_client_id to DefaultAzureCredential. Switching to system-assigned is tempting, but only one exists per resource.","Domain 4: DefaultAzureCredential with user-assigned identities",["Removing role assignments does not tell the credential which identity to use.","An app has only one system-assigned identity, so two identities cannot both become system-assigned.",null,"A client secret reintroduces a stored credential, which managed identities are meant to avoid."],3],["x48",0,4,"An app running in Azure with a managed identity unexpectedly authenticates as a service principal. The team finds AZURE_TENANT_ID, AZURE_CLIENT_ID and AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET set in its app settings from an old test. Why does this happen?",["DefaultAzureCredential tries environment credentials before managed identity","Managed identities are disabled whenever any AZURE_ prefixed setting exists","Service principals always outrank managed identities in Microsoft Entra ID","The managed identity token expired, so the SDK switched to the secret"],0,"DefaultAzureCredential tries EnvironmentCredential early, before ManagedIdentityCredential, so leftover secret variables win. Remove them to use the managed identity. Token expiry is tempting but the SDK renews tokens automatically.","Domain 4: DefaultAzureCredential chain",[null,"The identity is not disabled; the credential chain simply finds the environment credential first.","Entra ID does not rank principals; the SDK chooses which credential to use.","The SDK refreshes managed identity tokens; the switch is due to chain order."],3],["x49",0,4,"A compliance team requires that deleted Key Vault secrets can never be permanently removed until the retention period ends, even by an administrator. What should be enabled?",["Soft delete with a short retention","Purge protection on the vault","RBAC permission model on the vault","A private endpoint for the vault"],1,"Purge protection prevents purging deleted vaults and objects until the retention period expires, and it cannot be turned off once enabled. Soft delete is tempting since it enables recovery, but without purge protection a deleted item can still be purged.","Domain 4: Key Vault purge protection",["Soft delete alone lets a privileged user purge a deleted secret before retention ends.",null,"The permission model decides who can do what, but a highly privileged user could still purge.","Private endpoints restrict network access, not deletion behavior."],2],["x50",0,4,"A vault uses the Azure RBAC permission model. A deployment pipeline's identity must create and update secrets but should not manage keys or certificates. Which role fits?",["Key Vault Secrets User","Key Vault Administrator","Key Vault Reader","Key Vault Secrets Officer"],3,"Key Vault Secrets Officer can perform any action on secrets, but not keys or certificates. Secrets User is tempting because it is the usual app role, but it is read-only.","Domain 4: Key Vault RBAC roles",["Secrets User can read secret values but cannot create or update them.","Administrator covers keys and certificates as well, which is broader than required.","Reader sees vault metadata only and cannot read or write secret values.",null],2],["x51",0,4,"An app reads Key Vault secret DbPassword by a reference that includes a specific version ID. After rotation creates a new version, the app still uses the old password. What should change?",["Reference the secret without a version so the latest is used","Enable soft delete so old versions are removed automatically","Change the vault to the access policy permission model","Give the app's identity the Key Vault Administrator role"],0,"A versioned reference always returns that exact version. Omitting the version resolves to the current version (the platform refreshes versionless references periodically). Adding permissions is tempting but the app can already read the pinned version.","Domain 4: Key Vault secret versions",[null,"Soft delete governs deleted objects; it does not remove or bypass old versions.","The permission model affects authorization, not which version a reference resolves to.","Broader permissions do not change the fact that the reference pins an old version."],2],["x52",0,4,"A Container App needs a database password from Key Vault as a secret that the app can reference in environment variables, without copying the value into the app definition. What should be configured?",["An environment variable containing the Key Vault secret value copied at deployment","A Dapr component that writes the secret value into the image during the build","A Container Apps secret that references the Key Vault secret via a managed identity","A Key Vault access policy that grants the Container Apps environment read rights"],2,"Container Apps secrets can reference a Key Vault secret URI and use a managed identity with read access, and environment variables then use secretRef. Granting the environment an access policy is tempting, but the app still needs the reference and the identity.","Domain 4: Key Vault references in Container Apps",["Copying the value at deploy time stores it in the app definition and misses future rotations.","Baking secrets into images exposes them to anyone who can pull the image.",null,"Vault access alone does not make a secret available to the app; the app must define the reference."],2],["x53",0,4,"A release must ship a fixed, immutable set of App Configuration key-values so that the exact configuration used can be reviewed and reused later, even as keys change. What should the team use?",["A sentinel key with refresh","A label named after the release","An App Configuration snapshot","A feature flag for each key-value"],2,"Snapshots capture a point-in-time, immutable set of key-values that apps can load by name. Labels are tempting because they group keys by release, but the values under a label can still be changed.","Domain 4: App Configuration snapshots",["A sentinel key triggers refresh when changed; it does not freeze the values.","Label values can still be edited afterwards, so a labeled set is not immutable.",null,"Feature flags toggle features; they do not capture a fixed set of key-values."],3],["x54",0,4,"A team wants to turn a new checkout flow on for a subset of users and off again instantly, without redeploying the app. Which App Configuration capability fits?",["Feature flags","Snapshots","Key Vault references","Private endpoints"],0,"App Configuration feature flags, used with the feature management libraries, can turn features on or off and target subsets of users at runtime. Snapshots are tempting since they relate to releases, but they are immutable.","Domain 4: App Configuration feature flags",[null,"Snapshots freeze configuration; they are not for toggling features on and off.","Key Vault references point to secrets, not feature toggles.","Private endpoints secure network access and do not control app behavior."],1],["x55",0,4,"A Python app using the Azure Monitor OpenTelemetry Distro must record a custom count of orders placed so it can chart and alert on it. What should the developer use?",["A log message per order and a manual count in the Azure portal","A new Application Insights resource dedicated to order data","An availability test that calls the order endpoint every minute","An OpenTelemetry meter with a counter that is incremented per order"],3,"The OpenTelemetry metrics API (get_meter, create_counter, add) records custom metrics that the distro exports to Application Insights for charts and alerts. Logging each order is tempting but does not produce a metric by itself.","Domain 4: Custom metrics with OpenTelemetry",["Counting log lines by hand is not a metric and cannot drive alerts reliably.","A separate resource adds cost and still needs something to record the metric.","Availability tests probe uptime; they do not count business events.",null],2],["x56",0,4,"During a deployment, engineers want to watch request rates, failures and server health in near real time rather than waiting for normal ingestion. Which Application Insights feature fits?",["Sampling","Live metrics","Availability tests","Smart detection"],1,"Live metrics streams key metrics with about a second of latency, which is ideal during deployments. Availability tests are tempting since they also check health, but they run periodic external probes.","Domain 4: Application Insights live metrics",["Sampling reduces telemetry volume; it does not provide a real-time view.",null,"Availability tests probe endpoints from outside on a schedule.","Smart detection raises alerts about anomalies after analysis, not a live stream."],2],["x57",0,4,"Engineers want each failed request listed together with the exception type that caused it in Application Insights. Which KQL approach fits?",["Join requests with exceptions on operation_Id, then project the fields","Union requests and exceptions, then take the first 100 rows returned","Query exceptions only and summarize count() by bin(timestamp, 1h)","Query requests only, then render a timechart of the success column"],0,"Requests and exceptions from the same operation share operation_Id, so joining on it pairs each failed request with its exception. union is tempting because it combines both tables, but it does not correlate rows.","Domain 4: KQL joins",[null,"A union just stacks rows; it does not link each request to its exception.","Counting exceptions per hour loses the connection to individual requests.","A chart of successes shows trends but not which exception caused each failure."],3],["x58",0,4,"A team wants an alert when a Container App's average CPU exceeds 80% for 10 minutes, evaluated quickly with low cost. Which alert type fits?",["A log search alert on a custom KQL query","An activity log alert for the container app","A metric alert on the CPU usage metric","An Application Insights availability test"],2,"Metric alerts evaluate platform metrics such as CPU efficiently and near real time. Log search alerts are tempting because they are flexible, but they are better for conditions that need a KQL query over logs.","Domain 4: Metric vs log search alerts",["Log search alerts work but run queries on a schedule and cost more for a simple platform metric.","Activity log alerts fire on management operations, not resource utilization.",null,"Availability tests check endpoint responses, not CPU."],2],["x59",0,4,"A Container App revision fails to start, and the console logs are empty because the container never ran. Where should the developer look next?",["The ACR repository's tag history","The system logs (ContainerAppSystemLogs)","The Key Vault diagnostic audit logs","The Application Insights dependencies"],1,"System logs record platform events such as image pull failures, probe failures and scaling, which explain why a container never started. Application Insights is tempting but needs the app to be running to send telemetry.","Domain 4: Troubleshooting Container Apps",["Tag history shows which images exist, not why the platform failed to start one.",null,"Key Vault logs only help if the failure is about secrets, and they will not show platform start errors.","Dependencies are recorded by running code; a container that never started records nothing."],2],["x60",0,4,"A function app's identity was given the Cosmos DB Account Reader role in the portal's access control (IAM) blade, but data-plane reads using Entra ID fail with a forbidden error. What is missing?",["A Key Vault access policy for the Cosmos DB account keys","A higher consistency level configured on the Cosmos DB account","A private endpoint connecting the function app to Cosmos DB","A Cosmos DB built-in data role assignment, such as Data Reader"],3,"Cosmos DB data-plane access with Entra ID uses its own role definitions (Built-in Data Reader or Data Contributor) assigned through Cosmos DB SQL role assignments. Control-plane roles like Account Reader only cover management operations, which is the tempting mix-up.","Domain 4: Cosmos DB data-plane RBAC",["Key Vault policies affect key storage, and the goal is to read data with Entra ID, not keys.","Consistency level changes read guarantees, not whether a principal is authorized.","A private endpoint changes network paths; the error is an authorization failure.",null],3]]);
